{
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Legion",
          "informationUri": "https://github.com/Orthic-Labs/legion",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "a11y-progress-unnamed",
              "name": "Give each <progress> an aria-label or aria-labelledby pointing at the adjacent label span (job label / 'Downloading SAM 3' / 'Verifying SAM 3 model folder').",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Progress renders <progress max value /> with no accessible name (no aria-label/aria-labelledby/wrapping label); the visible text label is a sibling <span>, not associated. A screen reader user tabbing or browsing to the import/export/SAM download progress hears only an unnamed progress bar with a percentage and cannot tell which operation it reports."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ai-slop-bt2020-pq-constants-repeated",
              "name": "Hoist `BT2020_LUMA` and the PQ constants to module-level consts shared by pq_encode/pq_decode and the three encoders.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The BT.2020 luminance weights `[0.2627, 0.6780, 0.0593]` are declared as three separate local consts (hlg_signal, encode_avif_hdr, encode_png_pq), and the ST 2084 constants M1/M2/C1/C2/C3 are declared twice (pq_encode, pq_decode). The sibling BT.601 weights are likewise a fourth local K. A later correction to one copy leaves the HDR AVIF, HLG and PQ PNG paths with different luma weights, producing silently inconsistent HDR output between formats."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ai-slop-cli-orphan-doc",
              "name": "Move lines 180-181 down to sit directly above `fn library_error`.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The doc comment for library_error (\"Why `--library DIR` can't be opened ... issue #99\") was left stacked on top of library_warnings' own doc, so rustdoc/IDE hover shows a single merged comment on library_warnings and library_error (the function it describes) has no doc at all. Typical result of an edit that inserted a function between a comment and its item."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ai-slop-cli-test-comment-claims-undo",
              "name": "Either add an `edit.undo` step asserting the baseline exposure returns, or drop 'undo restores' from the comment.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The comment says the apply step checks that \"the values land, repeat-stable, undo restores\", but the test never runs an undo or asserts restored values; it only compares two applyLook results (the second with dryRun=true) and a finite exposure. A regression in undo of develop.applyLook would pass this test while its comment claims coverage."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ai-slop-desktop-windows-open-diverges",
              "name": "Share one implementation (the quoted raw_arg /select form and the rundll32 url.dll,FileProtocolHandler command) between the two hosts, or port both fixes and their tests into services.rs.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The Tauri desktop host re-implements open_url/reveal for Windows without the fixes the egui app already carries for the same OS calls. services.rs open_url runs `explorer <url>`, which apps/lightcraft/src/main.rs documents (issue #234) as opening File Explorer instead of the browser for links with a query or fragment (e.g. an OpenStreetMap map link). services.rs reveal passes `format!(\"/select,{path}\")` through Rust's arg quoting, which the same file documents (issue #272) as making Explorer open Documents instead of selecting the file whenever the path contains a space, and it does not strip the `\\\\?\\` verbatim prefix. On Windows, Reveal in Explorer on `C:\\My Photos\\a.jpg` or opening a link with `?`/`#` from the desktop app misbehaves; the egui app has regression tests for exactly these cases, the desktop host has none."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ai-slop-help-urls-duplicate-links-table",
              "name": "Serve the link table from the engine (or generate the TS map from links.rs) so there is one source of truth.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "HELP_URLS re-types the Help-menu link table that crates/ui-egui/src/links.rs already owns (LINKS/url_of), and the two have already diverged: app.help points to .../tree/main/docs here but to the repository root (HELP) in links.rs. The egui and desktop apps therefore send the same Help command to different pages, and any further URL change must be made twice."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ai-slop-path-validation-copy-paste",
              "name": "Extract one `valid_path_text(&str) -> bool` (or `check_path(&str, what)`) with a single named limit constant and call it from all sites.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The same path guard `path.is_empty() || path.len() > 8_192 || path.contains('\\0')` is pasted nine times across services.rs and native.rs with a bare 8_192 literal, while native.rs:770 already defines MAX_PERSISTED_LIBRARY_PATH = 8_192 that none of them use. Changing the limit or adding a rule (for example a newline or length-in-chars rule) means editing nine sites; sites missed will accept paths the others reject, and the error strings already differ (\"invalid path\", \"invalid dropped path\", \"invalid library path\")."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ai-slop-sam3-size-literal-triplicated",
              "name": "Have the host status always supply sizeBytes/validation.total and drop the TS fallbacks, or name one const in the TS file; unify the two Rust constants.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The SAM 3 weights size 3439938512 is a magic literal twice in DialogHost.tsx (validation total and size label) and again as separate Rust constants in crates/segment/src/fetch/mod.rs:31 and crates/engine/src/segment/mod.rs:34. If the model file is replaced, the Rust side and the dialog's fallback progress/size disagree (percent computed against the stale total, wrong 'about N GB' text) unless all four are edited."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ai-slop-toneft-doc-links-test-only-fn",
              "name": "Link to `search_ordered` (the production driver) instead.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The ToneFit doc says `[fit_pairs_ordered]` tries the fits in order, but fit_pairs_ordered is #[cfg(test)]-only; the production path is search_ordered. In a non-test build the intra-doc link does not resolve and the comment points a reader at a function that does not exist in the shipped code."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ai-slop.duplicate-stacked-doc-raw-samples",
              "name": "Collapse to one summary line plus the SHA-256 and per-variant notes.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "`RAW_SAMPLES` has four stacked doc lines, the first two restating each other ('CC0 raw samples (raw.pixls.us). Small, representative set; extend freely (CC0 only).' / 'CC0 raw samples from raw.pixls.us (each verified CC0 on the site).'): two generations of the comment were appended instead of replaced, so the rustdoc is duplicated and the instruction pair reads as contradictory scope ('small set' vs 'one per format / compression variant')."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ai-slop.orphaned-doc-comments-headless",
              "name": "Split each merged doc block and attach each paragraph to the function it describes.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "In headless.rs `HeadlessView::painted_shapes` carries two stacked docs ('Every piece of text the last frame painted...' then 'Every shape of the last frame...'), so the first describes the wrong function and `painted_text` (line 191) has no doc. In the test module the doc 'The folder scan runs in the background: the request returns at once and the review opens when the scan finishes' is merged onto `browsing_the_same_folder_again_does_not_restart` (line 2962) while `add_folder_scans_in_the_background` (line 2994), the test it describes, has none; the first test's intent becomes unreadable."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ai-slop.orphaned-doc-comments-i18n",
              "name": "Move the 'stock preset' paragraph onto builtin_label and the 'Locale tags' sentence onto locale_tags_normalize.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "`builtin_label` (i18n.rs:215) is undocumented while its doc 'A stock preset's or profile's name in the UI language...' is stacked on top of `startup_failed_message`'s doc, so startup_failed_message's rustdoc opens with preset-name text. In tests, 'Locale tags are matched leniently, so a system locale reaches a shipped language.' is merged onto `startup_failure_message_names_the_error_and_the_log` instead of `locale_tags_normalize` (line 495)."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ai-slop.orphaned-doc-comments-panels-and-corpus",
              "name": "Move each stray doc paragraph to the item it describes; remove the sentences that no longer belong.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Misattached docs: `fmt_gap` is documented 'Apply a dialog's action (also used by `ui.dialog.confirm`).' above its own '`90` -> `1 min 30 s`' line while `confirm_dialog` (dialogs.rs:1571), the function that sentence describes, has no doc; right.rs documents `copyright_status` with 'A labelled metadata text field: the typed text lives in egui memory...' while `meta_field` (right.rs:788) has none; menubar.rs `host_supports` ends with 'Restore and Delete Permanently replace Delete for photos in Recently Deleted', a sentence about a different item; corpus.rs documents `corpus_nef_high_efficiency_is_named` with the D7500 12/14-bit black-level paragraph, belonging to `corpus_nef_12_bit_black_level_matches_14_bit` (a test that has no doc). A reader of fmt_gap is told it applies dialog actions, and a failing corpus test points at the wrong stated intent."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ai-slop.orphaned-doc-comments-ui-lib",
              "name": "Move each doc paragraph onto the item it describes (toast, is_bw, album_counts) and delete it from slideshow_tick, HoverPreview and rules_view.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Doc comments in ui-egui/src/lib.rs are stacked on the wrong item, a copy-paste/merge tell. `slideshow_tick` carries 'Show a transient toast at the bottom of the canvas' while `pub fn toast` (line 720) has no doc; `HoverPreview` carries 'Whether the settings render in black & white' while `pub fn is_bw` (line 1389) is undocumented; the 'Every album's photo count for the sidebar... follow the clock within a minute' block sits above `rules_view` (merged with rules_view's own doc) while `album_counts` (line 1811) is undocumented. rustdoc then shows a wrong description for slideshow_tick, HoverPreview, rules_view and no description for toast, is_bw, album_counts."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ai-slop.test-only-helper-ignores-param",
              "name": "Drop the unused parameter and the tautological assert, and test the production sizing function (`loupe_edge` / `loupe_job`) directly.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "`loupe_request_dimensions` is `#[cfg(test)]`-only (no production caller) and accepts `_interactive: bool` that it never reads. Its test `interactive_loupe_request_keeps_full_display_dimensions` asserts `draft == full` between calls differing only in that ignored flag, which holds for any implementation, and the sizing it checks is a test-local reimplementation (`want = ...min(max_edge)`) rather than the production `AppSettings::loupe_edge` path, so the assertion cannot fail if the real loupe sizing regresses."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ai-slop.test-parses-serialized-json-text",
              "name": "Walk the typed MenuNode tree (as `sidebar_menu_items` does) to read the item's `enabled` flag.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "`resetting_the_sidebar_brings_every_section_back` decides whether the Reset item is enabled by serializing the whole menu bar to a string, `find(\"view.sidebarReset\")`, then splitting the rest of the text on `enabled\":` and checking `starts_with(\"true\")`. It reads whatever 'enabled' key follows the first textual hit, so a new field or reordered serialization (or the id appearing earlier in params/labels) makes it assert on another node's state; the typed `MenuNode::Item { enabled, .. }` walk is already used by `sidebar_menu_items` in the same file."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "arch-dialoghost-shadowed-form-variants",
              "name": "Delete the copysettings, pastesettings and cull entries from FormDialog's config and EXPLICIT_FORM_KINDS, leaving GroupsDialog and CullReviewDialog as the single owners.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "EXPLICIT_FORM_KINDS and the FormDialog config define copysettings, pastesettings and cull forms, but DialogHost routes those kinds to GroupsDialog and CullReviewDialog before reaching FormDialog, so the FormDialog variants (including their payload shaping and the 'Choose groups in Inspector' note) can never render. Two implementations of the same dialogs exist, and a maintainer changing the FormDialog version sees no effect."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "arch-duplicate-preferences-patch-paths-diverge",
              "name": "Route lc_native preferences.patch through the same helper as lc_preferences and decide once whether a missing host is an error after the preference file has been written.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Preferences are patched through two IPC entry points with copy-pasted bodies that disagree when the desktop host failed to start: lc_preferences writes ui.json via preferences.patch and then returns Err(startup_error), while lc_native action preferences.patch performs the same write and returns Ok(value). The frontend therefore reports a failed save for a write that actually persisted (or a success, depending on which entry point the caller used), and fixes to one path do not reach the other."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "arch-export-location-policy-two-authorities",
              "name": "Have export_metadata take the resolved removes_location(photo, catalog, o) result (or call it) instead of reading o.remove_location, so one function owns the location decision for all export formats.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "removes_location() is the declared seam for location policy ('photo and catalog let library-level privacy rules add to the export option') and the Original/DNG path (prepare_guarded, export.rs:1753-1755) goes through it, but export_metadata() for every rendered format reads o.remove_location directly (export.rs:1597, 1610). The moment a library-level rule is added to removes_location as its doc invites, Original/DNG exports drop GPS while JPEG/TIFF/PSD/WebP/AVIF/JXL exports still embed GPS and place names, leaking the location the rule was meant to strip."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "arch-qa-control-dispatch-parallel-command-surface",
              "name": "Extract each command body into one plain function taking (&AppState, AppHandle, caller label, args) and have both the #[tauri::command] wrappers and control_dispatch call it; keep only argument decoding in the QA adapter.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "control_dispatch re-implements every tauri_commands handler (lc_run, lc_snapshot, lc_preferences, lc_native and its action chain) instead of sharing them, and the copies already diverge: QA lc_snapshot never applies mask_snapshot_gpu when gpu_recovery is set, QA lc_preferences/preferences.patch never clear gpu_recovery, never apply preference_patch_for_window and silently succeed with no host where lc_preferences returns the startup error, and QA saveBeforeClose ignores the caller-window ownership rule. native_qualification.rs drives only this copy (control.command(\"lc_snapshot\"/\"lc_preferences\"/\"lc_native\")), so a GPU-crash-recovery or startup-failure regression in the production IPC handlers ships with a green native qualification run, and every new native action must be added in two dispatchers."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "arch-qa-mode-detection-ungated-in-release",
              "name": "Compute one RuntimeMode once at startup (behind cfg(feature = \"qa-native\") for every env-derived input) and pass it to build_shell, run(), lc_native and the macOS builder branch; release builds should compile the QA branches out.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "QA mode is derived in five independent places with different gating. runtime_modes deliberately ignores LIGHTCRAFT_DESKTOP_QA/RIGHTKIT_QA_HIDDEN/RIGHTKIT_QA_BACKGROUND unless the qa-native feature is built (default = [] in Cargo.toml), but unattended_qa_enabled, build_shell, the lc_native import-picker guard and the macOS activation branch read the same variables unconditionally. A release build launched with any of those variables set (e.g. inherited from a developer or CI shell) therefore uses the production library and preferences while build_shell sets show_on_ready(false) and run() sets focus=false, so the main window never appears, and native pickers are rejected as 'unavailable in hidden QA'. This also leaves QA control behaviour compiled into release builds rather than stripped."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "arch-session-catalog-public-bypasses-ops",
              "name": "Make Session::catalog read-only to frontends (pub(crate) field plus a &Catalog accessor) and route non-user bookkeeping through an engine API such as apply_system/commit so the documented ops-only invariant is enforced by visibility, not convention.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "lib.rs:7 documents the Catalog as 'mutated only by ops, so every change is undoable and journaled', but Session exposes it as a public mutable field. A frontend already bypasses it: desktop-host controller.rs:73 calls session.catalog.apply(Op::RemovePhoto) directly when --demo-count trims an opened library. That removal is not pushed to pending_log, not on the undo stack and skips apply_catalog's media.forget, so the in-memory catalog and the library's op log diverge (the photos come back on reopen, or a later snapshot/compaction persists the removal with no undo step)."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "arch-sync-pickers-bypass-parented-async-path",
              "name": "Make the async, parented path the only dialog path: drive every is_picker_action through one async picker function that takes the caller WebviewWindow, and derive is_import_picker/has_explicit_picker_input from one action table.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "services.rs documents that dialogs must be awaited from the caller's window on macOS instead of being dispatched synchronously from a worker, but the split is keyed on 'import' rather than on 'opens a dialog': pickPresetFiles, pickCurvePresetFiles, openFile, saveFile/saveExport/exportFile, savePresetFile, saveCurvePresetFile, pickTracklog, openLibrary, backupLibrary/restoreLibrary and selectFolder still run blocking rfd::FileDialog calls inside spawn_blocking with no parent window. These dialogs keep the unparented worker-thread behaviour the comment says to avoid (dialog not attached to the Ember window and able to open behind it), and the picker action list is maintained in four separate match tables."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "architecture.core:cycle:101cb6ca8108",
              "name": "Audit finding",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Provider reported a finding"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "architecture.core:cycle:13aacf6774c7",
              "name": "Audit finding",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Provider reported a finding"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "architecture.core:cycle:2803318e6970",
              "name": "Audit finding",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Provider reported a finding"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "architecture.core:cycle:f9e976e82956",
              "name": "Audit finding",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Provider reported a finding"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "correctness-cr3-framing-reference-fit-rounding",
              "name": "Resize the reference to exactly (w, h) as reference_at does (`resize(reference, w, h, Filter::Box)`) instead of fit().",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "estimate_cr3_framing fits the reference into the proxy's box with fit(), which keeps the reference's own aspect ratio, then returns None unless the size matches exactly. The comment on reference_at (398-402) records that this fit can round one pixel short (191x128 vs 192x128). When that happens, align_cr3_framing silently returns the unaligned sensor, so the CR3 framing correction is skipped for that file and the colour pairs are taken from misregistered pixels."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "correctness-credits-undated-sort-descending",
              "name": "Apply the direction only to the date comparison and keep undated entries last, e.g. compare (a.is_empty()).cmp(b.is_empty()) first and reverse only the date part when !ascending; add a descending date test.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "compare() orders undated (PR-only) contributors via dated()=(is_empty, date) so they sort after everyone with a date, as the comment at line 159 states. sorted() then applies o.reverse() for descending order, which also reverses that rule: with First/Last commit sorted descending (the ▼ toggle in the About window), every PR-only contributor with an empty date jumps to the top of the list ahead of the newest committers."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "correctness-decision-manifest-source-family-id-splits-virtual-copies",
              "name": "Derive source_family_id from the family root, e.g. the master's uuid for both the master and its copies (\"family:<root uuid>\"), and keep virtual_copy_identity as the per-copy identity. Add a test with one master plus one virtual copy that asserts both rows share source_family_id.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "source_family_id is computed with virtual_identity(), which returns \"original:<uuid>\" for a master photo (masterImage 0) but \"master:<master uuid>\" for each of its virtual copies. A master and its virtual copies therefore get different source_family_id values. The manifest's own split_policy says any later split must keep source_family_id groups intact, so a consumer that groups by this field can put the master in train and a virtual copy of the same image in test, leaking the same source pixels across splits. The field is also a byte-for-byte duplicate of virtual_copy_identity, so it adds no family grouping at all."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "correctness-dialog-confirm-errors-swallowed",
              "name": "Report every confirm_dialog error (at least a toast, ideally keeping the dialog open as for Export) instead of the catch-all close.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "In dialogs::show, a confirm_dialog Err is only reported for Import/Export/ContactSheet/NewAlbum/NewSmartAlbum/SmartRules/KeywordTag/KeywordSet/SynchronizeFolder (toast) and SamModel; every other Err falls into `_ => close = true`, dropping the message. app.run does not toast errors itself. Example: Edit Capture Time with a typed time that is not a date makes photo.setCaptureTime return `... is not a date`, and the dialog simply closes with nothing changed and no message; likewise Rename Photos (on-disk rename failure), Merge (start_final 'a merge is already running' / plan error), Rename Keyword, Label Names, Paste Settings, Cull and Delete failures vanish silently and the user's input is lost."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "correctness-hdr-maxfall-uses-luminance",
              "name": "Accumulate nits[0].max(nits[1]).max(nits[2]) into sum_nits (both the PNG and AVIF paths), matching the MaxCLL basis.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "MaxCLL is computed from the per-pixel max channel, but MaxFALL averages BT.2020 luminance. CTA-861.3 defines MaxFALL as the frame average of the per-pixel max(R,G,B); this is from the reviewer's recall and was not fetched. Saturated HDR content (a red highlight at 1000 nits has luminance of about 263) gets an understated MaxFALL in the PNG cLLI chunk and the AVIF content light level, which can make displays tone-map frames too brightly."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "correctness-hover-preview-key-omits-photo-identity",
              "name": "Fold the photo id and content key into preview_job's key the same way build_job does (or XOR the settings change into job.key instead of replacing it).",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "preview_job overwrites the key build_job made (which deliberately mixes in photo id and content: media.rs:1195-1197) with one from settings and size only. Two unedited photos from the same camera and aspect have identical develop settings, so hovering preset P on photo A and then stepping to photo B while still hovering P yields the same key. The UI's Renderer::request_needed (ui-egui render.rs:390-399) sees the Slot::Hover texture already holds that key and requests nothing, while the draw path filters that texture by photo (detail.rs:623): photo B never shows the hovered preset's look."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "correctness-is-unedited-ignores-wb-edits",
              "name": "Ignore only the as-shot case: reset wb to default only when wb.mode == WbMode::AsShot (keeping mode/temp/tint comparisons otherwise).",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The doc says is_unedited ignores only white balance 'as shot' values, but the code overwrites the whole wb (mode, temp, tint) with the defaults. Photo::is_edited is `*develop != defaults && !develop.is_unedited()`. So a photo whose only edit is a white-balance change (for example mode Custom at 3200 K) counts as unedited. It is missing from edited filters and smart rules, and lens_ops/import treat it as untouched."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "correctness-keywords-panel-overwrites-selection",
              "name": "Use addKeywords / removeKeywords (which the command already supports) for add/remove on a multi-selection, or send the replacing `keywords` list only for the active photo id; reload the shown list after the command instead of trusting local state.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "KeywordsPanel loads the keyword list of the active photo only (photo.inspect {id}), but add() and remove() send photo.setMeta {ids: snapshot.selection, keywords: next}. In the engine, `keywords` replaces the list (m.keywords.clear() then re-add) for every id. With photos A (active, keywords [beach]) and B (keywords [wedding, family]) selected, typing 'sunset' and pressing Add sets B's keywords to [beach, sunset]: B silently loses wedding/family and gains beach. Removing a chip likewise rewrites every selected photo to the active photo's remaining list."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "correctness-library-switch-keeps-photo-id-keyed-session-state",
              "name": "In open_stores clear every photo-id-keyed session field when the catalog is replaced (before, previous_active, target_album, folder_changes, active_mask/active_spot), alongside the undo/redo reset.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Photo ids are per library (library.rs:420, 512), and the desktop app switches libraries on the same Session (ui-egui panels/settings.rs switch_library: close_library then open_library). open_stores resets undo/redo/selection/source but not Session::before (HashMap<PhotoId, settings>) nor Session::previous_active. After using Before/After 'Set Before' on photo 12 in library A and switching to library B, photo 12 of B renders A's settings as its Before side, and beforeAfter.copyBeforeToAfter/swap commits those foreign settings onto B's photo 12. Likewise develop.pastePrevious in B copies settings from whatever B photo has A's previous-active id."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "correctness-lrcat-undo-merge-uses-capped-history-length",
              "name": "Capture s.commits() before the import and merge s.commits().wrapping_sub(before) steps (as sync.rs:633-639 does), instead of differencing undo.len().",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "LRCAT import commits the file additions (import::commit_prepared, one undo step) and then the metadata/album batch (a second step), and merges them by comparing s.undo.len() with undo0 captured at lightroom_job.rs:304. Session::commit drops the oldest entry once the history exceeds 1000 (lib.rs:516-519), so in a session whose undo history is already full (1000 steps from ordinary slider edits) undo.len() stays 1000 after both commits: merge_undo(0) is a no-op. The import is left as two undo steps; one Undo removes the ratings/edits/albums but keeps the added photos, a partial undo of an operation that is supposed to be one step."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "correctness-mcp-render-save-ignores-extension",
              "name": "When `path` is given and `format` is absent, derive the encoding from the path extension (as Headless::export and write_image do), or reject a path whose extension disagrees with the chosen format.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "render_photo always renders to a temporary .png and image_result re-encodes only when `format` is jpeg; it then writes those bytes to the caller's `path` without consulting the path's extension. An agent calling render_photo {path: \"/tmp/look.jpg\"} with no `format` gets PNG bytes saved under a .jpg name (screenshot behaves the same), while the headless app.export and ui.render paths pick the encoder from the extension."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "correctness-merge-failed-options-survive-reopen",
              "name": "Clear app.merge.failed_options in merge::open (and wherever app.merge.ids is replaced), or key the failed record on (ids, options) instead of options alone.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A merge preview fails for options X (spawn/plan error or the job returns Err) so app.merge.failed_options = Some(X). The user closes the dialog, selects a different set of photos and reopens the same merge kind: open() resets ids, error, preview and preview_pixels but never clears failed_options, and MergeDialog::for_command(command) yields the same default X. In poll(), stale = current != Some(opts) && !last_failed_matches(app, opts) is false because failed_options == X, so no preview is ever spawned for the new selection; with error cleared to None the preview box stays blank with no progress or message until the user changes an option."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "correctness-p9-auto-import-ignores-sync-and-catalog",
              "name": "In the done callback (and/or at ImportRun start in import::tick) refuse or defer when app.sync_run, app.lightroom or a non-browse scan is present, reusing busy_synchronizing; add a test that completes an auto-import listing while a sync run is active and asserts no ImportTask is created.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Auto Import checks import/sync_run/lightroom only when it spawns the folder listing (lib.rs:1054-1058). The listing runs on a worker (it may take seconds on a network share); its done callback re-checks only app.import.is_none() before installing an ImportTask, and import::tick starts the worker without checking sync_run or lightroom. If the user confirms Synchronize Folder (sync.rs only refuses when app.import is Some) or starts an LRCAT import (lightroom busy() only refuses when app.import is Some) while the listing is in flight, the auto import then starts alongside it. That violates the module's stated invariant that an import and a Synchronize Folder never run at once because each readies files against the library as it was when it started, so both can add the same file (duplicate catalog records); likewise an auto import can run during a catalog import's commit window."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "correctness-p9-rescan-drops-import-choices",
              "name": "Carry the current dialog's non-scan choices (copy, move_files, destination, organize, folder_template, rename, dng, album/new_album, preset, metadata_preset, keywords, on_deleted) through the rescan (e.g. store them on ScanTask, or merge them into the new ImportDialog in poll_scan), and add a headless test that rescans a device import and asserts Copy and the album remain selected.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Import from Device opens the review with Copy checked (menus.rs sets ScanTask.copy=true after import::open). If the user edits the scan filters and clicks Rescan, body() calls open_with_options, which always creates ScanTask{copy:false, album:None}; when that scan finishes, poll_scan replaces the whole dialog with ImportDialog::new(...) and sets d.copy=task.copy (false) and d.album=None. Move/destination/organize/rename/DNG/preset/keywords/new-album choices are also reset to defaults. A user who does not re-check the Transfer row then imports a memory card 'Add in place', so the library references files on removable media that go missing when the card is ejected; an album or keywords chosen before the rescan are silently not applied."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "correctness-p9-review-start-replaces-running-import",
              "name": "Make import::start refuse (or queue) when app.import is Some (non-browse), when a non-browse scan runs, or when lightroom_import::is_running(app), matching start_paths; add a test that confirms the review while an auto import is running and asserts the running task is preserved.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "import::start (the review dialog's OK / ui.dialog.confirm) checks only busy_synchronizing, unlike start_paths which refuses while app.import is Some or a review scan runs, and unlike lightroom busy(). With an Auto Import folder configured, an auto import (or a Local-view browse import) can start while the review dialog is open; confirming the dialog then assigns app.import = Some(new task), dropping the running ImportTask: its ImportRun Drop sets cancel, so remaining files are silently abandoned, any batches already sent but not yet committed are dropped without the Move rollback the worker performs on send failure, its committed batches are never merged into one undo step, and no completion/cancel toast is shown. Confirming during an LRCAT import likewise runs both at once although lightroom busy() treats an import as a conflict."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "correctness-preview-failure-cached-forever",
              "name": "Wire retry into the host command surface (or clear failed entries on file/volume change and after a time-to-live), or cache only deterministic errors.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A render error is cached in `failed` under (slot, job key), and later requests with the same key are answered from the cache without rendering (render.rs:198). The only way to clear it for a live slot is Renderer::retry, and nothing in the repository calls it. A transient failure (a file briefly offline on an unmounted volume, or a memory-pressure decode error) therefore keeps replaying the same error for that photo and slot until the slot is evicted or the edit changes the key, even after the cause is gone."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "correctness-reload-strips-duplicate-hash-suffix",
              "name": "Compare the base hash (strip the ':dupN' suffix, as missing.rs/sync.rs do) in content_op, and keep the suffix when the base hash is unchanged.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "photo.duplicate stores the copy's hash as '<hash>:dup<id>' (convert.rs:343), and other code strips that suffix before comparing (missing.rs stored_hash, sync.rs base_hash). content_op compares the fresh probe hash against the stored, suffixed hash directly. So Reload from Disk on an unchanged duplicate always reports it as changed: it adds an undo step, drops its cached sources, and writes SetContent with the bare hash. That removes the ':dup' marker the duplicate relied on to avoid clashing with its original's content hash."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "correctness-remote-stale-pool-gives-up",
              "name": "Treat an I/O failure on a pooled (reused) connection as a reconnect-and-retry on a fresh connection before counting a failure, or stamp pooled connections and drop them past an idle age below the server timeout. Consider resetting failures after a success.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Remote pools idle connections with no liveness check, while the server drops any connection idle past its 120 s read timeout (remote.rs:192). The engine keeps the loaded model, and so its Remote, for the next photo (denoise.rs `loaded`). If the user denoises a photo, waits more than 2 minutes, and starts another, every pooled connection is dead. Each try_run pops one, gets EOF ('the connection closed'), and counts a failure. With 4 slots, 3 failures reach MAX_FAILURES and the server is given up for the rest of the session: the remote GPU is never used again and the status shows 'given up', although the server is healthy."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "correctness-remove-panel-size-control-unused",
              "name": "Store the size in UI state (setUi) and pass it to spot.add when the drag is a click, or remove the dead control.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The Remove panel's Size slider and number field only update local state `size`, which is never read or sent; spot.add takes its size from the drag distance in StageWorkspace (Math.max(0.005, |dx| || 0.025)). A user who sets Size to 0.2 and clicks the photo still gets a 0.025 spot, so the control does nothing."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "correctness-segment-poll-drops-click-result-race",
              "name": "Decide idleness from the channel, not the counter: drain results first, then clear pending only if busy() is false and a second try_recv finds nothing; or send the reply before dropping the counter guard.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "segment_poll treats 'pending counter is 0' as 'every reply has been sent', then clears segmenter.pending. The worker decrements the counter (drop(done)) before it sends the reply (worker.rs:277-278). If the UI frame polls in that window, idle is true, try_iter returns nothing, and pending is set to None; when the reply arrives next frame apply_outcome's Tag::Clicks branch sees pending != latest and returns, discarding the segmentation. The user's Object-mask click silently produces nothing and must be repeated."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "correctness-shortcuts-cull-toast-ignores-failure",
              "name": "Have `cull` return the run result and only show the success toast when it is Ok (toast the error otherwise).",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The `cull` closure discards the result of app.run (line 400 only uses it for advance). The P/X/U and 0-5 key paths then toast 'Flagged as Pick' / 'Flagged as Reject' / 'Unflagged' / 'Rated ★★★' unconditionally (lines 435-440, 456-460). When the command fails (e.g. photo.pick's has_selection precondition with nothing selected, or a catalog write error), the user is told the photo was flagged/rated although nothing changed."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "correctness-shortcuts-x-crop-double-rotate",
              "name": "Make exactly one of the two fire entries act in the Crop panel: e.g. at line 509 `continue` without running (letting the crop.rotateAspect entry run), or skip the crop.rotateAspect entry when photo.reject is also in `fire`; add a test asserting X in Crop swaps the crop aspect exactly once and adds one undo step.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "photo.reject and crop.rotateAspect are both engine commands declared with shortcut X, so in handle() one X press pushes both ids into `fire` (bindable loop, lines 360-370). With the Crop panel open, the photo.reject entry is redirected to crop.rotateAspect (line 509-511) and the crop.rotateAspect entry is NOT skipped (line 513 only skips it outside Crop), so it falls through to app.run at line 559. Pressing X once runs Rotate Crop twice: the aspect is swapped and swapped back (net no visible change) and two 'Rotate Crop' undo steps are recorded. The existing test library_keys_yield_to_search_and_crop_context only asserts the flag stays None, not that the crop rotated once. (Declarations: crates/engine/src/cmd/library.rs:718 and crates/engine/src/cmd/develop.rs:784, both Some(\"X\").)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "correctness-xmp-iso-override",
              "name": "Delete the override at lines 512-514 (line 511 already reads exif:ISOSpeedRatings first), or apply the same >0 filter and fallback there.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "parse_xmp computes m.iso at line 511 with a >0 filter and an exifEX:PhotographicSensitivity fallback, then lines 512-514 unconditionally overwrite it with the unfiltered exif:ISOSpeedRatings value (parse_number already trims, so the override adds nothing but bypassing the filter). An XMP packet with exif:ISOSpeedRatings 0 (or a negative value) and a valid exifEX:PhotographicSensitivity of 6400 yields iso = Some(0) instead of 6400; a negative value saturates to Some(0) via `as u32`."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "doc-cli-mcp-connect-comment-describes-run",
              "name": "Rewrite the mcp comment to state that a non-address token is treated as a file (and rejected in connect mode), or share one connect-argument parser between run and mcp.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The --connect comment in mcp() says a non-address token such as a command id 'leaves the address at its default so the command is still consumed next', but mcp has no command tokens: such a token becomes a FILE and immediately fails with 'FILES, --demo and --library apply to headless mode only'. The comment was copied from run(), whose filter also differs (run rejects tokens containing '='), so the two parsers are documented as identical while behaving differently."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "doc-cli-module-header-omits-subcommands",
              "name": "Add the missing subcommands to the header or replace it with a pointer to USAGE.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The crate-level usage block lists subcommands but omits `look extract` and `ai compare`, both dispatched in main() and documented in USAGE, and omits denoise-serve's --max-connections. Readers of the source/rustdoc get an incomplete command inventory."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "doc-cli-orphaned-library-error-doc",
              "name": "Move the first two lines of the doc comment onto library_error.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The doc comment 'Why `--library DIR` can't be opened; for a library open in another program, how to work with that one instead (issue #99)' is attached to library_warnings, which only prints warnings; it describes library_error two items below. rustdoc and IDE hover show the wrong contract for both functions."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "doc-cli-render-extension-hint-stale",
              "name": "Generate the hint from the supported ExportFormat list, or update it to match USAGE.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "render's unknown-extension error tells users to 'use .jpg .png .tif .webp .avif .dng', omitting .jxl and .psd, which USAGE advertises for render and ExportFormat::parse (crates/engine/src/export.rs:36-50) accepts. Users following the error text are steered away from supported formats."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "doc-cli-run-output-ms-on-failure",
              "name": "Either include ms on failure lines or document that ms is present only when ok is true.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "USAGE promises one JSON line per command shaped {\"command\", \"ok\", \"result\" | \"error\", \"ms\"}, but a failed step is emitted as {command, ok:false, error} with no ms field. A script parsing the documented shape (e.g. summing ms) breaks on the first failure line."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "doc-desktop-gpu-crash-notice-wrong-on-macos",
              "name": "Make the notice platform-specific (metal/off on macOS, dx12/vulkan/off on Windows) and quote the actual Settings label.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "After a GPU startup crash the user-facing notice says to re-enable 'Settings > Performance > Use the GPU for rendering' and to try LIGHTCRAFT_GPU_BACKEND=dx12, vulkan or off. The Settings checkbox is actually labelled 'Use GPU rendering when available', and on macOS (a supported host for this binary) neither dx12 nor vulkan is a usable backend; metal, which parse_backends accepts (crates/gpu/src/backend.rs:11), is not mentioned. A macOS user following the recovery instructions sets a backend that cannot initialise."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "doc-drift-p7-corpus-pngsuite-not-downloaded",
              "name": "Either add PngSuite (pinned URL + sha256) to the corpus download into corpus/pngsuite, or remove the PngSuite claim from the USAGE text and the corpus.rs module doc and document how corpus/pngsuite is obtained.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "`cargo xtask` usage and crates/codecs/tests/corpus.rs both state that `cargo xtask corpus --download` fetches PngSuite into corpus/, but cmd_corpus only iterates RAW_SAMPLES into corpus/raw. corpus/pngsuite is never populated, so corpus_pngsuite always prints 'skip: ... absent' and returns; developers and CI believe PNG conformance coverage exists when it never runs."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "doc-drift-p7-sony-corpus-count-stale",
              "name": "Update the Sony doc counts/table to the current manifest, and add the ILCE-7CM2 and ILCE-7RM6 samples (expected 0 warps) to sony_embedded_distortion_is_limited_to_validated_models, or state explicitly why they are excluded.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "docs/sony-lens-corrections.md says the current `cargo xtask corpus --download` manifest holds 23 Sony samples across 17 bodies and that all of them are checked by sony_embedded_distortion_is_limited_to_validated_models. RAW_SAMPLES now also pins ILCE-7CM2 (2 files, issue #697) and ILCE-7RM6 ARW6 (2 files): 27 Sony files across 19 bodies. The 4 newer files are absent from the model-boundary test list, so the documented guarantee that every Sony corpus file is checked against the distortion model boundary is false for those bodies."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "doc-drift-p7-tests-masking-module-doc-scope",
              "name": "Move the unrelated tests into modules named for their features, or widen the module doc to describe what the file actually covers.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The module doc says tests_masking.rs holds headless tests of the Masking and Remove tools, but it also holds smart-album editor, slideshow, Local folder tree, external-editor, soft-proofing and 100k-grid performance tests. A developer changing those features searches the appropriately named modules, misses these regression tests, and the doc misdirects test ownership."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "doc-drift-p7-xtask-layering-doc-pointer-local-only",
              "name": "Point the xtask USAGE, cmd_layers header and layers.rs messages at docs/architecture.md#layers (the tracked document).",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "xtask usage, the `layers` report header and the unregistered-crate violation message all direct the reader to plan/architecture.md §3, but plan/ is git-ignored and docs/architecture.md states that file is local-only. A contributor on a fresh clone who hits 'register it in xtask/src/layers.rs TABLE (see plan/architecture.md §3)' finds no such file; the tracked rules live in docs/architecture.md '## Layers'."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "doc-drift-p7-xtask-usage-omits-version",
              "name": "Add `version [set X.Y.Z[-pre]]` to the USAGE command list.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "main dispatches `cargo xtask version [set X.Y.Z]` (the documented single source of truth for the workspace version used by packaging), but USAGE lists no `version` command, so `cargo xtask help` hides the version-bump entry point."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "doc-drift.corpus-sraw-allowlisted-though-decoded",
              "name": "Remove \"sraw\" from KNOWN_UNSUPPORTED (or narrow it to the specific non-YCbCr raw types still refused) so sRAW/mRAW corpus files must decode.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "KNOWN_UNSUPPORTED still lists \"sraw\" as 'Canon sRAW / mRAW' not decoded, but cr2.rs now routes SRAW_TYPE=4 frames to vendor::cr2::sraw::decode, measured on the 31 CC0 sRAW/mRAW corpus samples. The corpus test accepts RawError::Unsupported for any file whose name contains 'sraw' (corpus.rs:87), so a regression that makes every sRAW/mRAW file Unsupported again passes the corpus gate silently instead of failing."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "doc-drift.headless-painted-text-doc-misattached",
              "name": "Move line 177 to directly above `pub fn painted_text` at line 191.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The doc line for painted_text ('Every piece of text the last frame painted...') sits above painted_shapes, so rustdoc shows painted_shapes as returning text in paint order and painted_text (line 191) has no doc at all; Headless::painted_text (line 295) links to HeadlessView::painted_text expecting that description."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "doc-drift.raw-crate-doc-format-list-stale",
              "name": "Update the crate doc format list and unsupported list to match decode_with/is_supported, and drop 3FR from the OtherTiff variant doc.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The crate-level 'Formats' list omits containers decode() supports (Samsung SRW, Hasselblad 3FR/FFF via RawFormat::CfaTiff, Kodak DCS via RawFormat::KodakDcs) and its 'can't decode yet' list names only compressed ORF and CR3 variants, while corpus.rs:16 points to 'the crate docs' for a list that also includes Nikon HE NEF. RawFormat::OtherTiff's doc (line 115) still lists 3FR among undecoded TIFF raws although CfaTiff (line 109-112) says 3FR/FFF are read by the DNG reader. A maintainer or user reading the docs gets the wrong support matrix."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "doc-export-module-not-pure",
              "name": "Rewrite the module doc to state the actual boundary (pure encoders plus session-bound export orchestration, file I/O and post-processing), or move write_file/AfterExport/PreparedExport file reads into a separate module so the pure claim holds for what remains.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The module doc claims export is 'Pure (bytes in, bytes out) ... writing the file is the caller's job', but the module reads originals from disk (PreparedExport::run, export.rs:1817), owns the native file writer (write_file/write_file_durable, 1991-2008, creating folders and replacing files), and reserves/drives Apple Photos imports (after_export, 1954-1970). A caller relying on the documented purity (e.g. reusing it in the web build or a sandboxed context) gets filesystem and osascript side effects it was told it would not."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "doc-lens-profiles-only-embedded-stale",
              "name": "Update the comments in crs.rs and files.rs, docs/xmp-interop.md:138 and docs/gaps.md row 8 to cover database profiles; in crs::to_partial carry LensProfileEnable=0 over when the photo's correction came from the database rather than the file (or record the source of embedded_lens so the mapper can tell).",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "crs.rs:396-398, files.rs:47-49 ('These are the only \"profile\" corrections Ember applies'), docs/xmp-interop.md:138 and docs/gaps.md:29 ('No lens profile database') all say profile corrections are only the file's own embedded ones. Since lens_profiles::for_raw (lens_profiles.rs:63-71) a lensfun-format database (LIGHTCRAFT_LENSFUN_DIR) also fills Photo.embedded_lens, which turns optics.lens_profile on by default (catalog model.rs:418-420). The XMP mapper's rule, built on the stale premise, ignores LensProfileEnable=0, so with a database configured a Lightroom sidecar/LRCAT edit that had profile corrections off imports with database distortion/vignetting correction on: the geometry and the imported crop no longer match the source edit."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "doc-lrcat-existing-photos-preserved-overstated",
              "name": "Either gate the rating/flag/label/meta/captured ops on update_existing (or on the photo being unedited) to match the contract, or narrow the doc comment, command help and docs/lightroom-catalog-import.md to say only develop edits are preserved.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The import contract says 'Existing edited records are preserved unless explicitly requested' (lightroom_catalog.rs:743) and 'Existing edited photos are preserved unless updateExisting=true' (library.importLightroom, cmd/library.rs:1391). In apply_prepared only develop settings are kept (keep_develop, 864-914); for every photo already in the library it unconditionally emits SetRating, SetFlag, SetLabel, SetMeta (keywords replaced wholesale at 891, XMP metadata merged at 875) and SetCaptured (915-922). Re-importing a catalog with updateExisting=false therefore silently overwrites ratings, picks, labels, keywords, metadata and capture-time corrections the user made in Ember, contrary to the documented default (recoverable only by undoing the whole import step)."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "doc-misplaced-item-docs",
              "name": "Move each comment onto the item it describes (merge_undo, read_sidecars, apply_prepared) and give commits/MAX_SIDECAR_BYTES/ApplyPrepared their own one-line docs.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Doc comments sit on the wrong items, so rustdoc and IDE hovers describe the wrong API: Session::commits() (public) is documented as 'Fold the last n undo steps into one' while merge_undo(), which does that, has no doc (lib.rs:595-603); the description of the concurrent sidecar reader is attached to const MAX_SIDECAR_BYTES instead of read_sidecars (import.rs:645-648); 'Apply prepared normal-file results ... This function does no filesystem work' documents struct ApplyPrepared rather than fn apply_prepared (lightroom_catalog.rs:785-796)."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
