              "id": "min-civil-delegate",
              "name": "Delete import::civil and call lightcraft_catalog::dates::civil at the three sites.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "import::civil only forwards to lightcraft_catalog::dates::civil. view.rs:47 and cmd/manage.rs:139 already call the catalog function directly, so the engine carries a second public name for one function (callers: system_clock, files.rs:1059, tests)."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "min-expand-within-duplicates-expand-with-options",
              "name": "Make one walker parameterised by (limits, filter) returning (files, truncated); express expand and expand_with_options through it.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "expand_with_options (432-462) and expand_within (465-494) are the same loop: skip check, is_dir -> walk::files_in, push explicit files, dedupe with a HashSet. They differ only in the file filter and the log message. expand_within's only callers are expand() and one test, so the production import path runs one copy while expand() (used by browse, missing, ui import) runs the other."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "min-import-defaults-free-fn-unused",
              "name": "Delete the free function; callers already use Photo::import_defaults().",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "pub fn import_defaults(p) only returns p.import_defaults(). No caller of the free function exists in crates/, apps/ or xtask (every other hit is the Photo method or the Session field), so it is a dead public delegate."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "min-interaction-original-dead",
              "name": "Delete the `original` field and its initialiser; keep `photo` and `originals`.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Interaction.original is set from begin_interaction (lib.rs:845) but never read: no `.original` access on an Interaction exists in crates/ or apps/. end_interaction/cancel_interaction and cmd/develop.rs use only `originals`, whose first entry is the same Arc. The doc comment ('Active-photo edits keep photo/original for existing callers') names callers that do not exist."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "min-parse-gps-unused-param",
              "name": "Drop the `pos` parameter and the `let _ = pos;` line; call `one(&a, 'S')` and `one(&b, 'W')` with only the negative-hemisphere letter.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The `one` closure in parse_gps takes `pos: char` that is never used (`let _ = pos;` at line 1635 silences the warning); both calls pass a hemisphere letter that does nothing."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "min-quick-image-srgb-duplicates-embedded-preview-srgb",
              "name": "Extract `finish_preview_srgb(d, orientation_bytes, max_edge)` and call it from both.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "embedded_preview_srgb (724-732) and quick_image_srgb (989-997) end with the same five steps: fit to max_edge with Filter::Box when larger, drop alpha, preview_orientation(...), to_srgb8().oriented(...). They differ only in where the decoded image comes from and which bytes give the orientation fallback."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "min-read-sidecars-reimplements-workers",
              "name": "Replace the native branch with `let workers = workers(paths.len());` keeping the wasm `let workers = 1;` branch.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "read_sidecars recomputes available_parallelism().clamp(1, MAX_IMPORT_PROBE_WORKERS).min(n) inline, which is exactly workers(jobs) defined at import.rs:1523 under the same cfg(not(wasm32)). A change to the worker policy (cap, fallback 4) has to be made in two places."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "min-render-job-literal-repeated",
              "name": "Add a RenderJob constructor taking (photo, level, source, settings, request, key, cache, source_key) that fills the constant fields, and a helper for the variant cache/key pair.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Four builders (build_job, variant_job, face_job, Session::candidate_thumb_job) each write a 16-field RenderJob literal where request_id 0, stages/view_cache/display None, cache_generation and origin/info derivation are identical; variant_job and face_job also share the same cache+key tail (cache: Some((rendered, ck)), key: low^high of ck). Every new RenderJob field needs four edits."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "min-render-result-literal-repeated",
              "name": "Build the result from self.failed(..) (or one RenderResult::new) and override only `rendered`/`loaded`/`gpu_fallback`/`display`; bind the failed-result base before self.info is moved.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "run_unguarded spells the same 11-field RenderResult literal three times (cache hit, closing-before-render, closing-after-render) although RenderJob::failed (867) already builds it. Adding a RenderResult field means editing every copy plus failed() and QuickJob's two copies (1079, 1096)."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "min-source-total-duplicates-visible-filter",
              "name": "Extract `fn source_filter(&self, base: &Filter) -> Filter` doing to_filter plus the two source-specific fixups and call it from both.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "source_total re-implements the Folder and LibraryFolder branches of visible() (browse path/subfolders, library_folder default '.') verbatim. A change to how a source maps to a filter (e.g. the '.' default) must be made twice or the grid count and total diverge."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "minimize-detail-test-only-loupe-dimensions",
              "name": "Delete the helper and its call (keep the RenderJob draft/key assertions, computing dimensions inline or from the production path).",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "loupe_request_dimensions is #[cfg(test)] only, has no production caller, and ignores its `_interactive` argument. Its only test calls it with true and false and asserts the results are equal, so the test proves a helper that mirrors nothing shipped and can drift from the real request-size code without failing."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "minimize-dialogs-export-repeated-rendered-gates",
              "name": "Merge the adjacent `if rendered` blocks into one, and invert the empty-branch chain to `if rendered { if/else-if colour-space ladder }`.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The Export dialog body tests `rendered` in three back-to-back `if rendered {` blocks (export_size, sharpen, metadata/watermark) with unindented bodies, and an `if !rendered {} else if ...` with an empty first branch. The repeated gate hides that these are one conditional section."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "minimize-dialogs-hand-rolled-key-of",
              "name": "Replace the 4-line hasher dance in both functions with `let key = crate::key_of((template, start, app.session.catalog.revision, &ids));` and `crate::key_of((id, app.session.catalog.revision, px))`; drop the `use std::hash::{Hash, Hasher}` lines.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "rename_preview and watermark_preview each hand-roll DefaultHasher::new()/hash/finish to build a cache key, although the crate already ships key_of(parts) (lib.rs:1580, same SipHash default, hash_one). Three copies of the idiom exist; a change to the key scheme must be made in each."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "minimize:right-label-row-dead",
              "name": "Delete label_row (6 lines).",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "pub fn label_row has no caller anywhere in crates/ or apps/ (repo-wide grep finds only its definition); it is dead code kept alive only by being pub in a pub(crate)-reachable module, so it carries a duplicate of the label/value painting logic for nothing."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "minimize:state-sidebar-section-collapsed-test-only",
              "name": "Move it under #[cfg(test)] or have the tests call app.ui.sidebar.is_collapsed(SidebarSection::from_id(..)) directly.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "UiState::sidebar_section_collapsed is pub in production code but its only callers are tests_panels.rs and other tests; no non-test code calls it (the sidebar uses self.sidebar.is_collapsed directly), so it is a test-only accessor shipped in release builds."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "minimize:tests-has-helper-copied",
              "name": "Hoist has/click/exec and the T/SETTLE constants into one shared #[cfg(test)] helper module (e.g. tests_support.rs) and import it.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The same 3-line `has(h, id)` widget-presence helper (and near-identical click/exec/T/SETTLE helpers) is redefined verbatim in 11 tests_*.rs modules (tests_panels, tests_album_picker, tests_activity, tests_date_picker, ...), so a change to how widgets are looked up must be repeated in every copy."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "naming-app-discord-points-to-github",
              "name": "Rename the ids/constants to what they open (e.g. app.community, app.organization) with an alias table for persisted shortcuts, or document the legacy ids at the HELP_URLS definition.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The command id `app.discord` (and the `DISCORD` constant in links.rs, labelled \"legacy constant retained for callers\") opens GitHub Issues, not a Discord community; `app.artcraft` opens the Orthic Labs GitHub org and `app.website` the repository. A reader or agent grepping for the Discord link or the website finds the wrong destination, and the id promises a channel the product no longer has."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "naming-fit-tone-family-meanings-collide",
              "name": "Name by method and result, e.g. fit_tone_quantile, fit_tone_conditional, and monochrome_tone_look for the CameraLook-returning one.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "`fit_tone` (quantile fit returning a CameraTone), `fit_tone_conditional` (the other CameraTone fit), `fit_tone_with_shadows` (a tone fit variant) and `fit_tone_only` (returns a whole CameraLook with the colour kept) share a prefix but not a return type or meaning; `fit_tone` does not say it is the quantile method although ToneFit::Quantile exists, and `fit_tone_only` reads like the generic tone fit. A caller picking `fit_tone_only` expecting a CameraTone fails to compile at best, or picks the monochrome-only path by mistake."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "naming-headless-two-types-one-file",
              "name": "Alias on import (`use lightcraft_ui_egui::headless::Headless as UiHeadless`) or qualify one of them.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "main.rs imports `lightcraft_mcp::Headless` (an in-process MCP backend session) at module level, then `snapshot()` imports `lightcraft_ui_egui::headless::Headless` (a UI renderer) under the same name, shadowing it; the test at 1055 also uses a bare `Headless::demo()`. Two unrelated types share one identifier in one file (and the CLI also uses mcp's `Remote`, a control-connection backend, while denoise's `Remote` is a tile runner), so adding a second use in either scope silently binds the wrong type."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "naming.crop-and-mask-overlay-name-collision",
              "name": "Rename the drawing functions for what they draw (e.g. `crop_box_tool`, `mask_handles`) and keep `*_overlay` for the guide/overlay setting.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "In panels/detail.rs `fn crop_overlay` draws the crop box and its handles, while `app.ui.crop_overlay` (state.rs:560) is the guide style (thirds/grid/spiral) and `super::crop_overlay::paths` (line 1398, inside that very function) is a third `crop_overlay` module; `fn mask_overlay` draws outlines/pins/brush while `ui.mask_overlay` (state.rs:550) toggles the rendered overlay of the mask (its own doc says the mask itself shows as `view_overlay`). The same word names four different things in one file, so a reader or grep for 'crop_overlay'/'mask_overlay' lands on the wrong concept and an edit to the toggle gets applied to the drawing function."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "naming.left-panel-folder-polysemy",
              "name": "Prefix by section (`album_folder_menu`, `library_folder_menu`, `local_folder_row`, `folder_display_name`) so each name identifies the sidebar section it serves.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "'folder' means three things in left.rs: `folder_menu(.., a: &Album)` is the context menu of an album folder, `folder_menu_for_library(.., n: &FolderNode)` (with sibling `row_menu`) is the library Folders row menu, and `folder_tree` is a Local on-disk folder row while `folder_rows`/`folder_row` draw the library Folders section (and `Caches::folder_tree` in lib.rs:1587 is the library tree, not the Local one). Separately `folder_label(path)` returns a display name while `folder_label_menu` is the colour-label submenu. A change to 'folder_tree' or 'folder_menu' is easy to apply to the wrong sidebar section."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "naming.pick-lightroom-catalog-typed-pick-folder",
              "name": "Type it with a name that means a single-path picker (e.g. a `PickFile` alias for `FnMut() -> Option<String>`) and reserve `PickFolder` for folder choosers.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "`pick_lightroom_catalog` is typed `Option<PickFolder>` (alias doc: 'A folder chooser') but the only implementation calls `.pick_file()` with a `.lrcat` filter and returns a file path. The alias is used for a file picker, so a new host (web/Linux/test) reading the type will implement a folder dialog and hand back a directory that the importer then rejects, and `PickFolder` no longer means 'folder'."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "naming.ui-commands-fn-vs-const",
              "name": "Rename the function to `all_ui_commands` (or name the constant `NON_LANGUAGE_UI_COMMANDS`) and update parity doc text.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "`menus::ui_commands()` iterates LANGUAGE_COMMANDS then UI_COMMANDS, so the function named `ui_commands` returns a superset of the constant `UI_COMMANDS`. Callers that need every UI command must use the function while anyone grepping or reading `UI_COMMANDS` (xtask/src/parity.rs:4 cites 'a UI command from `UI_COMMANDS`') silently misses the language commands."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0001-01",
              "name": "Move the eval and corpus subcommands to a shared tools/research crate, or gate them behind a non-default feature, and keep run/mcp/render/snapshot/merge/commands in the shipped CLI.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The shipped lightcraft-cli (packaged by release.yml and the FreeBSD package script) links about 5,500 lines of research harnesses (cull_eval 1550, personal_auto_eval 1525, decision_manifest/extract/plane, denoise_eval, measure_batch, cull_timing). Their own usage text calls them experimental and they take NEW_* private corpus paths. Every user build compiles and ships them, and their rayon/geom/color/raw deps exist only for them."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0001-02",
              "name": "Extract one synchronous dispatch function over (AppHandle, action, params, caller_label) and call it from both the Tauri command and the QA control plugin. That drops roughly 60 duplicated lines.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "control_dispatch's lc_native arm re-implements the whole lc_native action ladder (openLibrary, backup/restore, preferences.patch, saveBeforeClose, window actions, model folders, import pickers) a second time for QA control. The two copies already differ (QA saveBeforeClose has no caller-label handling), so a fix to one silently misses the other."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0001-03",
              "name": "Add an AppState::host() -> Result<DesktopHandle, String> helper and route the preferences.patch action through the shared lc_preferences body.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Every Tauri command opens with the same host/startup_error clones and the same `host.as_ref().ok_or_else(|| error.unwrap_or_else(|| \"desktop host is unavailable\"...))` expression, repeated about 10 times in this file. The lc_preferences body and the `preferences.patch` branch of lc_native are near-identical copies, so a change to GPU-recovery handling must be made twice."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0001-04",
              "name": "Define one `fn valid_path_str(s: &str) -> bool` (or a `checked_path` returning Result) in services.rs and reuse it from services.rs and native.rs.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The path-sanity predicate `path.is_empty() || path.len() > 8_192 || path.contains('\\0')` is pasted at 8 sites with 8 differing error strings, plus a MAX_PERSISTED_LIBRARY_PATH constant that duplicates the same 8_192. Changing the limit needs 9 edits."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0001-05",
              "name": "Collapse each picker pair to the async variant, or one shared builder, and drop the unreferenced aliases from run, is_import_picker and is_picker_action.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "services.rs keeps a sync picker path (pick_files, pick_files_with_params, run's dialog arms) and a duplicate async path (pick_files_async, pick_files_with_params_async, run_import_picker) with identical extension filtering and limit checks. Many action aliases (pickPhotos, importFolder, pickDevice, importDevice, importLightroom, saveExport, exportFile, import) have no caller in web/src, native.rs or the engine. Only the path-only contract test touches some of them."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0001-06",
              "name": "Delete the three diagnose_* probes and keep the existing failure-layout.json, failure-dom.json and failure-native.png capture.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "diagnose_native_viewport_bounce, diagnose_stage_child_invalidation and diagnose_same_parent_layout (about 340 lines of ad-hoc DOM probe scripts) run only on macOS inside the failure path of with_control. They are leftovers from chasing one layout-bounce bug. They never assert anything and only write evidence on an already-failed run."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0001-07",
              "name": "Factor `parse_connect(args, &mut i)` and `open_headless(library, demo, imports)` helpers and call them from both subcommands.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "mcp() and run() each hand-parse `--connect [ADDR]` (identical port/addr heuristic), `--demo`, `--library`, and the same Headless-open sequence (open_library, library_error, library_warnings, import paths). snapshot() repeats the Session builder chain a third time. A fix such as the #1178 connect heuristic has to be applied to each copy."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0001-08",
              "name": "Add `clampPreviewEdge`/`clampMemoryMb` helpers and a `runAndRefresh(id, params)` helper.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The previewEdge clamp `Math.max(512, Math.min(16384, Math.round(x)))` and the memoryMb clamp `Math.max(0, Math.min(1_048_576, ...))` are each written out 3 to 4 times across migrateLegacyUi, clampUi and preferenceUi. In `run`, the `await runCommand(...); await refresh(); return value;` tail repeats about 12 times."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0002-001",
              "name": "Delete the unused method or document the external consumer that calls it.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Dead public accessor if no external host consumes the engine crate; removing it or confirming the external consumer closes the finding."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0002-002",
              "name": "Remove the alias or name the host that depends on it.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unused exported alias in a pub module; it adds API surface with no consumer."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0002-003",
              "name": "Wire the caller that runs the deferred archive finalization, or delete the function.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unreached associated function; if the deferred archive finalization was meant to run on a worker, that path is missing and the work never executes."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0002-004",
              "name": "Delete it or add the caller that renders the compact token hint.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unused exported helper in a pub module."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0002-ai-slop-dead-param-pos",
              "name": "Either drop the `pos` parameter and the `let _ = pos;` suppression, or use it (reject a coordinate whose letter belongs to the other axis).",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The closure `one` in parse_gps takes a `pos: char` hemisphere letter (callers pass 'N' and 'E') that is never read; `let _ = pos;` only silences the unused warning. The positive hemisphere letter is therefore not validated: a latitude typed as \"51.5 E\" or a longitude as \"0.1 N\" is accepted silently as positive instead of rejected."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0002-ai-slop-misplaced-doc",
              "name": "Move the \"Fold the last `n` undo steps into one\" paragraph down onto merge_undo and leave commits() with only its own \"How many undo steps were committed\" doc.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The doc comment that describes merge_undo (\"Fold the last `n` undo steps into one...\") is stacked on top of the doc for commits(), so rustdoc renders one run-on doc on Session::commits() and Session::merge_undo, the function it describes, has no doc at all. A reader of the generated docs or an agent reading the signature is told commits() folds undo steps."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0002-albumdialog-identical-ternary",
              "name": "Replace the ternary with the literal 'album.create'.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "AlbumDialog calls desktop.run(folder ? 'album.create' : 'album.create', ...): both branches are the same string, so the conditional is dead logic that suggests a distinct folder command that does not exist."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0002-arch-desktop-host-startup-policy-duplicated",
              "name": "Move the launch-option parsing and startup policy (library resolution, demo or in-memory semantics, GPU crash sentinel and notice) into one shared owner, such as lightcraft-engine or desktop-host, that both hosts call. At minimum, give `--demo` the same meaning in both hosts.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The two desktop hosts (egui `apps/lightcraft` and Tauri `apps/lightcraft-desktop`) each re-implement startup policy (CLI flags, library resolution order, GPU crash sentinel, crash notice text) instead of sharing one owner, and the copies have already diverged. In the egui host `--demo` is an alias of `--memory`, which its help text describes as a throwaway in-memory session where nothing is saved. In the Tauri host, `--demo` still resolves the user's real library (from the env var, the saved path or the default) and opens it on disk with seed_demo=true (controller.rs:58-61). A user or script that relies on the documented `--demo` throwaway mode ends up opening and writing their real library when they run the Tauri build. The GPU crash notice is also duplicated word for word (main.rs:175 and native.rs:950), so a wording or guidance fix has to be made in both places."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0002-camera-preview-colour-closure-copied-three-times",
              "name": "Extract one `fn apply_colour(matrix: &Mat3, correction: Option<&HueSat>, x: [f64; 3]) -> [f64; 3]` (or a CameraLook method) and call it from all sites.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The `matrix.apply(x)` followed by optional HueSat correction closure is copy-pasted in fit_pairs_on (852), fit_tone_only_on (1101) and fit_chroma (1307, with a slightly different borrow shape). fit_partial_on (998) uses a fourth, correction-less variant. A change to how a look's colour is applied must be made in each, and a missed copy silently fits tone against a different colour model than the one rendered."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0002-camera-preview-rank-correlation-duplicate",
              "name": "Keep only rank_correlation_ties (renamed rank_correlation) and call it from fit_partial_on too; re-run the corpus look tests and bump LOOK_VERSION if any accepted look changes.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "rank_correlation and rank_correlation_ties are two near-identical 20-line Spearman implementations; the second only adds tie handling (mean rank) and a symmetric variance guard. fit_partial_on still uses the weaker one while fit_tone_only_on and fit_tone_with_shadows use the tie-aware one, so quantized-luminance JPEGs are scored two different ways against the same PARTIAL_MIN_RANK_CORRELATION threshold."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0002-dialoghost-unreachable-form-kinds",
              "name": "Delete the 'cull', 'copysettings' and 'pastesettings' entries from EXPLICIT_FORM_KINDS and from FormDialog's config map, the matching payload branches in submit, and the two trailing Note/Check JSX fragments for those kinds.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "FormDialog carries config entries, payload branches and extra UI for 'cull', 'copysettings' and 'pastesettings' (and lists them in EXPLICIT_FORM_KINDS), but DialogHost routes those kinds earlier to CullReviewDialog (line 1009) and GroupsDialog (lines 1023-1024) before reaching the EXPLICIT_FORM_KINDS check at line 1029, so every one of those FormDialog branches is unreachable. A maintainer editing the cull or copy/paste form there changes nothing users see."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0002-doc-library-env-precedence",
              "name": "Update the module docs and the comment at main.rs:824 to give the actual order: --library, EMBER_LIBRARY, LIGHTCRAFT_LIBRARY, saved library, then the default.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The module docs of the desktop app say the library comes from `--library`, else `$LIGHTCRAFT_LIBRARY`, else the last opened library. The resolver checks `EMBER_LIBRARY` first and falls back to `LIGHTCRAFT_LIBRARY` (library.rs:74-76). The inline comment at main.rs:824 leaves out the environment step entirely. A user who has both variables set (or only EMBER_LIBRARY) and reads the module docs or the comment will mispredict which library opens. The `--help` text in the same file does list both."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0002-doc-pastemetadata-fields-undocumented",
              "name": "List every copied key (altText, extendedDescription, city, state, country) in both spec strings, or have `location` expand to location, city, state and country in the paste filter.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The spec for photo.copyMetadata lists only title, caption, copyright, creator, location and keywords. The code also copies altText, extendedDescription, city, state and country (library.rs:628-630). The `fields` enum for photo.pasteMetadata leaves those five keys out, yet the filter matches raw clipboard keys (library.rs:649). An agent that follows the spec and passes fields:[\"location\"] to paste a place gets only the sublocation string; City, State and Country are silently left alone. Alt text and city/state/country can be pasted selectively only by guessing undocumented key names."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0002-doc-photo-label-missing-param-clears",
              "name": "Return an error when `label` is absent, as folder.label does, or document `label?` with absent meaning none.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The photo.label spec marks `label` as required (`{label: red|...|none, ids?}`). The code treats a missing `label` the same as \"none\" (library.rs:733), so a control or agent call that misspells or omits the key (for example {\"color\":\"red\"}) succeeds and removes the colour label from every selected photo, with no error. The sibling folder.label command returns \"missing `label`\" for the same mistake (library.rs:491)."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0002-library-source-and-removefolder-duplicate-validation",
              "name": "Extract `fn folder_ids(s, path, command) -> Result<Vec<PhotoId>>` for the shared checks and keep the per-command policy (disk flag) at each call site.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "library.source (libraryFolder arm) and library.removeFolder repeat the same sequence: reject blank/non-folder path via folder_key, reject is_startup_disk, build Filter{library_folder}, query, reject empty result with the identical message, then covers_other_disks. The two copies already differ in wording and in the disk:true escape hatch, so a fix to one rule (for example the startup-disk check) can miss the other."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0002-main-redundant-second-startup-marker",
              "name": "Delete the second `if !in_memory { begin_startup_marker(...) }` block in the creation closure.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "begin_startup_marker is called before run_native (line 858) and again inside the eframe creation closure (line 933). MarkerTracker::begin_startup_marker only arms when startup_active is false, and nothing between the two calls clears startup_active (startup_succeeded runs on the first frame, after the closure), so the second call is a no-op that reads as if it re-armed the sentinel."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0002-rules-field-menu-triple-list",
              "name": "Add a group column (or Option<&str> group) to FIELDS and derive TOP_LEVEL_FIELDS/FIELD_GROUPS/field_group from it; the sync test then collapses to a uniqueness check.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The same 50 rule-field ids are written three times in the same order: FIELDS (with comment headers naming the groups), TOP_LEVEL_FIELDS and FIELD_GROUPS. A test (every_field_is_in_the_menu_exactly_once) exists only to keep the three lists in sync, which shows the duplication is a maintenance hazard: adding a field needs three coordinated edits or CI fails."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0002-settings-repeated-typeof-boolean-chain",
              "name": "Add a small helper such as `pick = (key, check, fallback) => [supplied, persistedUi, persistedSettings].map(o => o[key]).find(check) ?? fallback` and call it per key; this removes roughly 10 long lines and the drift hazard.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "SettingsDialog builds `initial` with a hand-repeated `typeof supplied.X === 'boolean' ? supplied.X : typeof persistedUi.X === 'boolean' ? ... : typeof persistedSettings.X === 'boolean' ? ... : desktop.ui.X` chain for confirmDelete, autoAdvance, filmstrip, filmNames, filmBadges, gridInfo, navigator, gpu (each about 250 characters, text/num variants likewise). Adding a setting means copying a 4-way chain again; one has already drifted (confirmDelete and gpu skip persistedSettings while the others consult it)."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0003-dead-file-001",
              "name": "Either wire the model-folder selection flow to verify_file, or delete verify_file and correct the doc comment.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "No call site for verify_file exists anywhere in the repository (repo-wide word search finds only its definition). Its doc comment says the model-folder selection flow calls it so manually supplied model files get the same size and SHA-256 checks. No such caller exists, so the promised check never runs on that path and the doc describes behavior the code does not have."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0003-dead-file-002",
              "name": "Confirm an external consumer exists and document it, or delete the method.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The method cull_burst has no caller anywhere in the repository (repo-wide word search finds only its definition). The provider-culling path it documents, including its frozen-rate budget reserve and receipt handling, is unreachable from any code in this repo."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0003-dead-file-003",
              "name": "Wire current_wb into the UI display path that needs it, or delete it.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "current_wb has no caller anywhere in the repository (repo-wide word search finds only its definition). Its doc comment says it exists for UI display of the effective temperature and tint, but no UI path reaches it, so the displayed value comes from somewhere else or is not shown."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0004-001",
              "name": "Delete `with_preprocess` if no external consumer is intended, or add the caller and a test that exercises its validation.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "`YuNet::with_preprocess` is a public builder that replaces the detector's preprocessing policy (scale, mean). A repository-wide search finds no caller in production code, tests, or examples (`crates/segment/examples/yunet_qualify.rs` uses `YuNet::detect_rgb` and fixed preprocessing only), so the setter is unreachable from any route, CLI, or test and its validation branch is never exercised."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0004-002",
              "name": "Remove the constant or reference it from the code that uses the 640x640 tensor side.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "`pub const INPUT_SIZE` in `lightcraft_segment::yunet` has no reference anywhere in the repository (code, tests, examples, or docs), so it is an orphaned export that a reader may take as the configured input size while the real size is fixed by the private `INPUT_SIDE`."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0004-003",
              "name": "Delete `label_row` or call it from the panel that needs a label/value row.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "`label_row` is a `pub fn` in `panels::right` with no caller in the workspace (searched all .rs files; the only match is its definition). It draws a label/value row that no panel renders, so it is dead code unless an external crate consumes `lightcraft_ui_egui::panels::right`."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0004-corpus-skip-boilerplate",
              "name": "Add one helper such as fn corpus_file(dir: &Path, name: &str) -> Option<Vec<u8>> that reads the file, prints the skip line on NotFound and panics on other errors, and use it in every test; expected net cut of about 60-80 lines.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "crates/raw/tests/corpus.rs repeats the 'read corpus file, else eprintln skip: <name> absent and continue/return' block at 22 sites (two spellings: let-else Ok and a match on ErrorKind::NotFound), roughly 4-8 lines each. Each new corpus test copies it again, and the spellings already differ on whether a non-NotFound IO error panics (match form) or is silently skipped (let-else form)."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0004-correctness-mask-id-truncation",
              "name": "Convert with `u32::try_from(v)` and treat an out-of-range id as `no mask {id}` (bad params) in mask_id, the mask.add/update lookup at line 260 and mask.select.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Mask ids from command/MCP params are narrowed with `v as u32`, which wraps instead of rejecting. `mask.delete {id: 4294967297}` (2^32+1) resolves to mask id 1 and deletes (or renames/inverts/moves via the same mask_id helper) a different, existing mask than the caller named, instead of returning `no mask 4294967297`. mask.select (line 545) likewise selects an unrelated mask. This contradicts the repository rule that input-derived numbers are hostile and must use checked conversion."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0004-correctness-placenames-nonatomic-replace",
              "name": "Write each file to the staging folder and rename into place (or use lightcraft_catalog::safe_file::write_atomic), replacing the cities file last.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The download's own contract (line 183) says the new place-name files replace the old ones only when complete, but each file is written in place with std::fs::write (truncate then write). If the disk fills or the app quits while writing the ~cities file (line 204), the previously working gazetteer is replaced by a truncated one. metadata.placeNamesStatus then reports `downloaded: true` (it only checks the file exists), and Fill Location from GPS silently finds fewer or no places. The optional files are also written (line 199) before the cities file, so a failure there leaves a mix of old and new data."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0004-correctness-rename-start-overflow",
              "name": "Validate `start` (e.g. 0..=999_999 like export's start_number, via usize::try_from and a range check) and return bad params otherwise.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "photo.rename / photo.renamePreview take `start` as any u64 and cast it with `as usize`, with no upper bound. plan_rename_core iterates `(start..).zip(photos)`. With start = 18446744073709551615, RangeFrom::next overflows on the first step. In debug builds that panics: the command fails through the catch_unwind net rather than with a bad-params error. In release builds the sequence wraps to 0, 1, …. On 32-bit wasm, the `as usize` cast also silently truncates the value. This breaks the project's never-crash rule for hostile numeric input."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0004-correctness-staging-filename-collision",
              "name": "Stage each download under a per-id name (e.g. `.downloads/<id>/<file_name>` or prefix the id), or refuse to start a download whose staged file name is in use by another running or finished job.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Downloads::start only refuses a second download of the same model id; different models download side by side into one shared staging folder keyed only by spec.file_name. Catalog entries (catalog.json) derive file_name from the URL basename and fall back to `model.onnx`, so two catalog models can share a name and write the same `.downloads/<name>.part`. Concurrently, they corrupt each other's partial file and both fail. If one finishes and the second starts before the next faces.pump, the second fetch reuses or overwrites `<name>`. finish_downloads installs the first id with `verified: true` and its recorded sha (no rehash, face_models.rs:265). That can install the other model's bytes under the first model's manifest, or leave the first job's Done path pointing at a file that is gone."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0004-engine-tests-group-validation-dup",
              "name": "Drive the shared cases from one table: a helper taking (command, params-builder) that runs the invalid-name and invalid-type lists and asserts BadParams plus zero mutation, then keep only the command-specific happy-path assertions per test.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "develop_paste_validates..., develop_sync_validates..., develop_copy_and_paste_previous_validate_groups and presets_validate_groups_on_create_and_update re-state the same sequence (bogus group names, a 5-7 entry list of wrong-typed JSON, valid subset, omitted, explicit null) with only the command name changed; paste and sync are near line-for-line copies of about 70 lines each. A new group-taking command needs another 70-line copy."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0004-headless-tempdir-boilerplate",
              "name": "Provide one shared test helper (a test-support crate fn or the tempfile crate's TempDir with auto-cleanup) and replace the 30 inline sites and the per-module temp_dir copies.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "headless.rs open-codes std::env::temp_dir().join(format!(\"lc-...-{pid}\")) + remove_dir_all + create_dir_all in 30 tests, and the same helper is separately redefined as fn temp_dir(tag) in about 19 other test modules (engine tests_*.rs, tests_preset_import.rs, tests_keyword_files.rs, preview/src/tests.rs, catalog/src/tests_lock.rs). Each also hand-rolls cleanup, so a failing assert leaks the directory and a stale pid-named dir can collide."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0004-resilience-1",
              "name": "Pass --connect-timeout, --max-time and --retry to curl, download to `<name>.part` and rename after the sha256 matches, and keep going after a failed sample, reporting all failures at the end.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "`cargo xtask corpus --download` runs curl with no --connect-timeout/--max-time/--retry, writing straight into the final path. A stalled connection hangs the command indefinitely; a mid-transfer failure leaves a truncated file at `out`. The `?` aborts the whole run, and the next run sees `out.exists()`, skips the download, fails the sha256 check, deletes the file and errors again; only the third run actually re-downloads. One transient network error therefore costs two failed runs, and one bad sample stops every later sample from being fetched."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0004-resilience-2",
              "name": "Delete any stale keychain (`security delete-keychain \"$KEYCHAIN\" 2>/dev/null || rm -f \"$KEYCHAIN\"`) before creating it, and have the EXIT trap restore the original search list and delete the keychain on failure.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The script creates a keychain at a fixed path ($TMP/lightcraft-signing.keychain-db), prepends it to the user's keychain search list, and its EXIT trap removes only the .p12. If the script is run again on a machine whose TMP persists (a self-hosted runner or a local run), `security create-keychain` fails because the file exists and `set -e` aborts the signing job. Any failure after line 36 also leaves the temporary keychain on the search list."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0004-schema-placenames-undocumented-base-param",
              "name": "Either remove `base` from the release command surface or add it to the contract with its constraints.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "metadata.downloadPlaceNames reads a `base` parameter (the download address, line 221) and rejects non-https values with \"`base` must be an https:// address\". Its published contract (line 286) declares only `{dataset?: folder}` and states the data comes from download.geonames.org. Schema-driven callers cannot know the parameter exists, and the contract's claim about the source is not enforced by the implementation."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0004-schema-renamepreview-contract-claims-mutation",
              "name": "Change the description to say it returns the planned renames (`[RenamePlan]`) without touching files, and document the returned shape.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The published command contract for `photo.renamePreview`, a non-journaled `query` that only returns plan_rename's plans, is copied verbatim from photo.rename. It says the command renames the files on disk (sidecars too) and is undoable. An MCP agent or UI that chooses commands from this schema text may treat the preview as the mutation: it reports files as renamed when nothing changed, or avoids calling the preview because it believes the preview is destructive."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0004-security-download-pinning-invariant-drift",
              "name": "Correct the invariant to say catalog.json entries are user-supplied addresses verified by their declared hash/size (or restrict them to https).",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The downloader's stated security invariant says only a model in lightcraft_faces::known::download can be fetched, with the address a pinned constant that is never typed or passed in. download_spec falls back to entries from the user-editable catalog.json, whose `url` is arbitrary. So the app fetches from addresses that are neither built in nor pinned. Integrity still holds via the catalog's required sha256/size, but the documented trust boundary that reviewers and agents rely on is false."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0004-security-keyword-export-overwrites-originals",
              "name": "Call `s.check_write_target(path)` before writing (as export.contactSheet does) and write atomically.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "keyword.export writes the keyword list with std::fs::write to any caller-supplied `path` (UI, CLI or MCP agent) and never calls Session::check_write_target. The app's stated invariant (originals.rs:1-5) is that files Ember writes for the user never land on a catalogued photo's original or XMP sidecar. Other writers enforce it (image exports via OriginalGuard, export.contactSheet, merge). `keyword.export {path: \"/Photos/IMG_0001.xmp\"}`, or the original raw path, silently truncates and replaces that file with a text keyword list. That destroys the photo's sidecar edits and metadata, or the original itself."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0004-security-placenames-unpinned-base",
              "name": "Drop the `base` parameter from release builds (keep it behind cfg(test)/debug for the loopback test server) or pin the GeoNames files by size/hash.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "metadata.downloadPlaceNames accepts an undocumented `base` parameter that may be any https:// host (base_allowed only checks the scheme). The fetched files carry no size or SHA-256 pin (`size: None, sha256: None`). They are then written as the gazetteer into `dataset`, which is also a caller-chosen folder. An agent or script driving the command interface can make the app fetch from an arbitrary host and install unverified place data. Reverse geocoding then writes that data into photo metadata (City/State/Country) across the library. This contrasts with the face-model downloader, which pins address, size and hash."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0005-arch-faces-pump-twice-per-frame",
              "name": "Remove one of the two calls (keep a single per-frame faces pump in logic_inner).",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "logic_inner calls panels::faces::pump twice in the same frame (after slideshow_tick and again after model_setup::pump). The second call's faces.pump engine step is mostly suppressed by faces_next_pump, but watch_downloads is not gated: while a face-model download is watched, the engine command faces.models.downloads is executed twice per frame, and input-time bookkeeping runs twice. Duplicate orchestration of one subsystem in the per-frame loop."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0005-arch-language-table-triplicated",
              "name": "Generate LANGUAGE_COMMANDS and language_from_command from Locale::ALL (e.g. a command-id column in language_table!), so the menu, Settings and control channel share one ordered source.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A UI language is declared three times by hand: the language_table! entries (Locale::ALL order: ... Pt-BR, Es, De, Ru, Fr, Uk), LANGUAGE_COMMANDS (order: ... Pt-BR, Fr, Es, De, Ru, Uk) and language_from_command. The copies have already diverged in order: Settings > Language lists French after Russian (Locale::ALL), while Edit > Language lists it right after Portuguese (LANGUAGE_COMMANDS registry order). Adding a language means editing all three sites in step, and the coverage test only checks membership, not order."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0005-arch-ui-command-dispatch-sprawl",
              "name": "Split run_ui_command by command family (view/panel/dialog/file/app) and add a test that every UI_COMMANDS id resolves to Some(..) in run_ui_command, or move to a typed command enum that carries label, shortcut, handler and enablement together.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "UI commands are a string table (UI_COMMANDS, 158 ids) whose behaviour lives in a single ~1,525-line match in run_ui_command, with enabled state in a separate string match (ui_enabled) and checked/live-label state in menubar.rs. Nothing ties the table to the handlers at compile time: an id added to UI_COMMANDS but missed or misspelled in run_ui_command falls through `_ => return None` to the engine and fails as an unknown command at runtime, and menu enablement defaults to `true` for any id ui_enabled does not name. All 158 current ids are handled today (checked), so this is a change-risk/shotgun-surgery finding, not a live break."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0005-doc-locale-all-menu-order",
              "name": "Either reorder one table to match the other (preferably derive LANGUAGE_COMMANDS from ALL) or correct the doc to say ALL is the Settings order.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Locale::ALL is documented as 'Every language, in menu order', but the Edit > Language menu is built from LANGUAGE_COMMANDS, whose order differs (French follows Portuguese there; in ALL it follows Russian). The doc claim is false and the two language pickers present different orders."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0005-doc-misplaced-docs-i18n",
              "name": "Move the two stock-preset lines down onto builtin_label.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The doc for builtin_label ('A stock preset's or profile's name in the UI language; an imported or user-edited name stays exactly as its owner wrote it') is stacked on top of startup_failed_message's own doc, so startup_failed_message documents two unrelated things and builtin_label (a public translation-policy function) has no doc at all."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0005-doc-misplaced-docs-lib",
              "name": "Move each orphaned doc line to its item: the toast line to toast(), the black & white line to is_bw(), and the album-count paragraph to album_counts().",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Three rustdoc comments in lib.rs are attached to the wrong item: slideshow_tick is documented as 'Show a transient toast at the bottom of the canvas' (a leftover from toast()); the HoverPreview struct is documented as 'Whether the settings render in black & white' (belongs to is_bw, which has no doc); and rules_view carries album_counts' whole description ('Every album's photo count for the sidebar...') while album_counts at line 1811 is undocumented. Generated docs and IDE hovers describe the wrong behaviour for these items."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0005-doc-misplaced-test-doc-export",
              "name": "Move the doc comment to export_dialog_runs_in_the_background.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The doc 'The Export dialog hands its batch to a worker thread: the UI keeps drawing frames, shows progress, and reports the result when the files are written' sits on export_dialog_keeps_its_width (a dialog-width stability test for issue #8), while the test it describes, export_dialog_runs_in_the_background, has no doc. A reader triaging a failure gets the wrong intent for both tests."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0006-arch-dialog-dispatch-scattered",
              "name": "Move large inline bodies (Export, KeywordTag, KeywordSet, CaptureTime) into their own functions or modules, like Merge, Import and Settings. Consider gathering per-dialog metadata (title, width, window id, Return-confirms, keeps-open) into one method on Dialog, so adding a variant takes one exhaustive match instead of several partial ones.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Each Dialog variant is defined in state.rs (780-1018), but its behaviour is spread over separate exhaustive or partial matches in dialogs.rs: title (223-265), width (289-293), body (299-1120, inside the 1,143-line show() function), confirm_dialog (1571-1751), return_confirms (1946-1948), window_id (176-182) and keeps_open (1416-1418). Some dialogs delegate their body to their own module (Merge, Import, Settings, Sync, Denoise, Faces). Export instead renders 327 lines inline in one match arm (660-987). The partial matches (return_confirms, window_id, keeps_open, width) fall through silently, so a new dialog that needs Return-to-confirm, its own window id, or a stay-open rule compiles without them. The scattering already shows in the confirm_dialog doc comment, which has drifted onto fmt_gap (1400-1402)."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0006-arch-favorite-themes-strict-field-fails-whole-file",
              "name": "Make the field tolerant: deserialize Vec<String>, then drop unknown ids and duplicates (as the keymap does) instead of returning an error. Keep strict validation for the control-channel write path if that is where it is wanted.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "favorite_themes uses a validating deserializer that returns an error for any unknown or duplicate theme id. An error in one field fails deserialization of the whole UiState. decode_saved_ui recovers only the docking field (main.rs:226-240), so load_prefs_at treats the whole ui.json as damaged: the file is renamed to ui.json.corrupt-* and every setting reverts to defaults, including library path, keymap, panel layout and appearance (main.rs:263-279). This happens when a ui.json written by a build with an extra or renamed theme id is opened by another build (downgrade, web vs native, or a theme removed later), or when the file is hand-edited. That is the opposite of the persistence policy the same file follows for the keymap, where a bad entry is ignored (shortcuts.rs:54-57)."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0006-arch-renaming-component-persisted",
              "name": "Mark renaming_component #[serde(skip)] like renaming_mask, or reset it in sanitized(). Consider splitting UiState's transient fields into a non-serialized struct so new transient fields can't be persisted by mistake.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "UiState mixes saved preferences with per-frame transient state, separated only by per-field #[serde(skip)]. renaming_component (an inline mask-component rename in progress) lacks the skip that its sibling renaming_mask (state.rs:621-622) has, so it is serialized. PrefsWriter::save_ui writes ui.json whenever the serialized state changes (main.rs:332-350), so starting a rename writes Some((mask_id, index, text)) to disk, and sanitized() (1209-1226) does not clear it. After a quit or crash mid-rename, the next launch restores it. masking.rs:309-315 then shows a rename field on any photo with a mask of that id and component index, and request_focus() on every frame takes keyboard focus away from other widgets until the user presses Esc or leaves the field. The stale text is also exposed through ui.get."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0006-correctness-geocode-high-latitude-misses",
              "name": "Widen the longitude search to ceil(MAX_KM / (111.32 km x cos(lat))) cells (clamped, with wrap) instead of a fixed +-1.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "reverse() searches only the 3x3 block of 1-degree cells. One degree of longitude is 111 km x cos(lat), under MAX_KM (50 km) above about 63.3 degrees latitude, where cities15000 does list places (Tromso 69.6N, Murmansk 68.9N, Reykjavik 64.1N, Fairbanks 64.8N). A photo at 69.65N, 18.02E sits in cell 18; a place at 69.65N, 16.95E is about 41 km west, within MAX_KM, but lies in cell 16 (dx = -2) and is never examined, so the lookup returns a farther place in the 3x3 block or nothing."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0006-correctness-smart-migrate-same-folder-deletes",
              "name": "Before removing src, skip when src and dst are the same file (compare canonicalized paths or (dev, inode)/file id), and have smart_location compare canonicalized directories rather than PathBuf equality.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "migrate(Move) deletes the source when a valid proxy already exists at the destination. The only same-folder guard is the lexical `new_dir == current` PathBuf comparison in smart_location (previews.rs). On macOS/Windows case-insensitive volumes, or via a symlink or a `..` spelling, the user can pick the same folder under another spelling (e.g. `/Users/a/Pictures/smart previews` for `/Users/a/Pictures/Smart Previews`). Then for every proxy dst resolves to the same file as src, is_valid(&dst) is true and std::fs::remove_file(&src) deletes it: every smart preview is lost and counted as 'moved'. Smart previews are the only editable copy while originals are offline, so photos on an unplugged drive become uneditable."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0006-correctness-sync-undo-interleave-at-cap",
              "name": "Track interleaving with the monotonic s.commits() counter (record commits after each own step and flag interleaved when it differs on entry) plus an undo-pop counter, instead of undo.len().",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "SyncCommit detects an Undo or edit made between its own steps only by comparing undo.len() with the length after its last step. Session::commit caps the undo stack at 1000 (lib.rs:516 removes the oldest entry), so once the history is full every commit leaves undo.len() unchanged; likewise an Undo followed by one edit restores the same length. In the app the SyncWork runs on a worker while the UI thread keeps accepting edits: with a full history, import batch committed -> user edits a photo -> Relink step committed, after_last == undo.len() so interleaved stays false, and finish() merges the last `own` (2) entries = [user edit, relink] into one 'Synchronize Folder' step. Undoing Synchronize Folder then also reverts the user's unrelated edit, contrary to the documented contract that an edit made meanwhile stays its own step."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0006-doc-confirm-dialog-doc-on-fmt-gap",
              "name": "Move the first line onto pub fn confirm_dialog and leave fmt_gap with only its own one-line doc.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The comment 'Apply a dialog's action (also used by `ui.dialog.confirm`).' sits directly above fmt_gap and merges into its rustdoc. fmt_gap only formats seconds ('90' → '1 min 30 s'). The function the comment describes, confirm_dialog (1571), is public and the control channel's confirm entry point, yet it has no doc. Readers of fmt_gap's doc are told it applies dialog actions, and the confirm contract is undocumented."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0006-doc-denoisemodel-doc-says-system-info",
              "name": "Move the System Info doc onto SystemInfo, and give DenoiseModel its own doc (the AI Denoise model dialog: model info and the licence 'I accept' box).",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The doc comment on Dialog::DenoiseModel reads 'Help ▸ System Info: (label, value) rows.', but the variant holds {info, accepted} for the AI Denoise model licence and install dialog (rendered by panels::denoise::model_dialog, confirmed by denoise::install). The real SystemInfo variant (919-921) has no doc. Rustdoc and editor hovers send readers to the wrong dialog."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0006-doc-meta-field-doc-on-copyright-status",
              "name": "Move lines 771-772 above fn meta_field at line 788, leaving only the Copyright Status line on copyright_status.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The doc for meta_field ('A labelled metadata text field: the typed text lives in egui memory while focused and is saved (photo.setMeta `key`) when the field loses focus.') is stacked onto copyright_status, a ComboBox that saves immediately on selection. The result is one contradictory doc on copyright_status, while meta_field (788) has none. Anyone reading copyright_status's doc expects focus-loss saving that it doesn't do."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0006-doc-sam-help-points-to-upstream-repo",
              "name": "Point SAM_HELP at the Ember docs, via a constant in links.rs (for example HELP + '/blob/main/docs/ai-masks.md#getting-the-model'), so one module owns the project URL.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Every user-facing project link is centralized in links.rs and points to the Ember repository (github.com/Orthic-Labs/ember: HELP, GITHUB, FEEDBACK). The SAM 3 install guide link in the dialog is hard-coded to the upstream repository instead: storytold/lightcraft/blob/main/docs/ai-masks.md. Ember ships its own docs/ai-masks.md with the 'Getting the model' section, which describes Ember's own dialog text. A user following the in-app manual-install guide gets the upstream project's instructions, which can diverge from Ember's (model folder, mirrors, env vars), or a dead link if upstream moves the file."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0006-doc-settings-tabs-list-stale",
              "name": "List every tab id (general | import | performance | display | interface | faces | denoise), or point the doc at settings::TABS as the source of truth.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Dialog::Settings documents `tab` = general | import | performance | interface. settings::TABS (settings.rs:21-29) also has display, faces and denoise, and the dialog body dispatches on them. A control-channel client or agent following the variant doc (Dialog is set and read through ui.set/ui.get) will never open the Display, Faces or AI Denoise tabs, and will treat those values as invalid."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0006-doc-tests-masking-module-scope",
              "name": "Move the unrelated tests into matching tests_* modules (for example tests_smart_albums.rs, tests_crop_tool.rs), or widen the module doc to match what is there.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The module doc says the file holds headless tests of the Masking and Remove tools. The file also covers the crop tool (464-540), keyword sets (542-561), the smart-album rule editor (563-800+), the Local folder tree, slideshow, external editors, grid performance and soft proofing. Someone looking for smart-album or crop coverage won't find it under the matching tests_* module, and new tests in those areas may duplicate it."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0006-schema-catalog-download-url-filename-contract",
              "name": "Download catalog entries from their exact url with lightcraft_fetch::download_url (which keeps the query and takes the local name from FileSpec), or reject at catalog parse time any url whose path does not end in `/<file_name>` with no query.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Download.base() assumes the url ends in `/<file_name>`, an invariant enforced only by the built-in table's test (known.rs:199). User catalog entries break it: catalog::entry accepts any https url and derives file_name by stripping the query and filtering characters, and face_download passes spec.base() to lightcraft_fetch::download, which requests `<base>/<file_name>`. For the module's own documented shape `https://example.org/w/recogniser-r50.onnx?download=1` (catalog.rs test), strip_suffix fails, base() returns the whole url and the request becomes `.../recogniser-r50.onnx?download=1/recogniser-r50.onnx`. A url without an .onnx name (`https://host/files/123`) gets file_name `model.onnx` and fetches `https://host/files/123/model.onnx`. Signed or query-bearing catalog urls therefore cannot be downloaded, though the catalog accepts them."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0006-security-tls-truncation-accepted-as-eof",
              "name": "In BodyMode::Close over TLS, treat UnexpectedEof or a read_tls of 0 without close_notify as HttpError::Protocol (truncated), or require Content-Length or chunked for unpinned files; pinning sizes and hashes for vocab.json and merges.txt also closes it.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "For https, a TCP close without TLS close_notify (rustls UnexpectedEof), and a raw read_tls of 0, are both returned as a clean end of stream. Length and Chunked bodies catch this, but a response with neither Content-Length nor chunked encoding uses BodyMode::Close, where the 0 marks the body as Done. A network attacker who injects a FIN/RST can then truncate the body undetected. fetch_file keeps unpinned files (size None, sha256 None) once len > 0, and segment/src/fetch/mod.rs:20-21 downloads vocab.json and merges.txt exactly that way, so a truncated merges.txt is installed as complete and silently degrades the tokenizer."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0007-total-ram-fallback",
              "name": "Delete total_ram_gb and its or_else at line 90; let ci_jobs(None, cpus) use its existing default of 4 when available RAM is unknown (about 35 lines removed).",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "total_ram_gb is a second per-OS probe (/proc/meminfo, powershell, sysctl) that only runs when available_ram_mb already failed, and halves installed RAM as a guess. On Linux and Windows the same file/PowerShell source that failed for available RAM is very likely to fail here too, so the fallback is largely dead flexibility; only a macOS vm_stat parse failure reaches it usefully."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0012-correctness-corpus-pngsuite-never-downloaded",
              "name": "Add PngSuite (pinned URL + sha256) to cmd_corpus --download, or correct the usage text and corpus.rs header and make the PNG corpus test fail rather than skip where coverage is expected.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "`cargo xtask corpus` documents that --download fetches PngSuite and CC0 raw samples, and crates/codecs/tests/corpus.rs says corpus/pngsuite is fetched by that command, but cmd_corpus only iterates RAW_SAMPLES into corpus/raw. corpus/pngsuite is never created, so corpus_pngsuite (including the never-panic check on deliberately corrupt x* PNGs) silently skips on every machine and CI run that relies on the documented download, leaving PNG decoder corpus coverage permanently unexercised while appearing to pass."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0012-correctness-static-check-substring-contracts",
              "name": "Replace the substring checks with behavioural tests that exercise the bounds (queue capacity, id range, cache eviction, stale-sequence rejection), or relabel the step as a presence lint so it is not counted as contract coverage.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The desktop CI gate's 'static contracts' step reports bounded IPC/cache/stale-preview contracts as enforced by checking only that source files contain identifier substrings (sync_channel, MAX_JS_SAFE_ID, MAX_PENDING, DEFAULT_TTL, descriptor.sequence, MAX_SLICE = 512). A change that removes the bound enforcement while keeping the constant name or a comment mentioning it still prints 'desktop static contract: PASS (... IPC/cache contracts)', so the gate claims behavioural guarantees it never exercises (false coverage)."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0012-security-appimagetool-continuous-unverified",
              "name": "Download a pinned appimagetool release by tag and verify a recorded SHA-256 before chmod +x and execution (also when reusing the cached file).",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "When appimagetool is not on PATH the release packaging script downloads it from the moving `continuous` GitHub release with no version pin or checksum, executes it, and the AppImage it produces embeds that tool's runtime, which runs on every user's machine. A changed or compromised continuous build alters the shipped AppImage runtime without any change in this repository, and the cached copy in CARGO_TARGET_DIR is reused without re-verification."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0012-security-sign-unpinned-trusted-signing-client",
              "name": "Pin the package version in the URL and verify a recorded SHA-256 (or the package's NuGet signature) before extracting and loading the dlib.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Azure Trusted Signing downloads whatever version of Microsoft.Trusted.Signing.Client nuget.org currently serves as latest, with no version pin, hash or signature check, then loads Azure.CodeSigning.Dlib.dll into signtool while AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET and the other signing credentials are in the environment. A changed or compromised latest package executes arbitrary code with release-signing credentials, and an upstream breaking release silently changes the signing path of a release build."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "p0012-security-web-serve-windows-absolute-path",
              "name": "Reject rel values containing ':' or any non-Normal path component (use Path::components and require Component::Normal only), and canonicalize the result and require it to start with the canonical serve dir; optionally validate the Host header.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The `cargo xtask web --serve` static server only rejects `..` components and backslashes. On Windows a request such as `GET /C:/Users/<u>/.ssh/id_rsa` yields rel `C:/Users/...`; Path::join with a path carrying a drive prefix replaces the base, so dir.join(rel) reads any file on disk and returns it with 200. The server binds 127.0.0.1 but performs no Host validation, so any local process, or a remote page via DNS rebinding, can read arbitrary developer files while the dev server runs."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "perf-album-order-quadratic",
              "name": "Build a HashMap<PhotoId, usize> from `order` once and sort with `sort_by_cached_key`, or use `sort_by_key` over that map.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Viewing a manual album sorted by capture date with the default filter re-sorts `visible` with `order.iter().position(..)` as the sort key. `position` is a linear scan of the album's photo list, and `sort_by_key` calls the key function O(n log n) times, so the sort costs O(n*m*log n). A 40k-photo album does on the order of 10^10 comparisons. `visible()` recomputes whenever the catalog revision changes, so every edit, rating or flag change in that album stalls the command or UI thread for seconds."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "perf-album-vec-contains-quadratic",
              "name": "Build a HashSet of the existing/target ids once per command and use it for membership tests.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "`album.addPhotos` tests `photos.contains(&t)` against the album's Vec for every target, `album.removePhotos` and `album.toggleTarget` test `targets.contains(..)` / `al.photos.contains(..)` per album photo, and select-by-criteria with `add` tests `ids.contains(&id)` per visible photo. Each is O(album x targets). Importing into an album calls `album.addPhotos` once per batch of 8-16 photos against the growing album (`import_batch_done`). Adding 20k photos to a 20k-photo album, or a 40k-photo import into one album, costs hundreds of millions to billions of comparisons on the command thread. Each call also clones the full album Vec into `SetAlbumPhotos` and its undo inverse."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "perf-filmstrip-clones-and-walks-whole-view-each-frame",
              "name": "Use visible_shared and compute the visible index range from the scroll viewport instead of walking all ids.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "filmstrip() copies the whole visible id list (visible_cloned = visible().to_vec()) and iterates every id each frame to compute a rect and an intersect test, though only about a dozen cells are on screen; the loupe prefetch at line 499 does a second full clone plus a linear position() scan per idle frame. The engine already offers visible_shared (Arc<[PhotoId]> with a generation). At 100k photos this is a per-frame O(N) allocation and walk."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "perf-gainmap-apply-serial-triple-work",
              "name": "When gain.channels == 1 compute one sample and one powf per pixel and reuse it for the three channels; hoist the per-row y coordinate; and parallelise apply() and compute() by rows with lightcraft_raster::par_rows as the neighbouring code does.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "gainmap::apply walks every pixel on one thread and, for each pixel, runs three bilinear gain.sample calls (four slice reads each) and three powf plus three exp2 even for the single-channel maps the encoder writes by default (channels==1 maps src=0 for all three channels, so the same sample and powf are computed three times). compute() likewise runs one log2 per pixel (three when multichannel) serially. On a 24 MP HDR gain-map export or decode this is on the order of seconds on one core while to_srgb8 and the JPEG encoder in the same pipeline use all cores, against the project's 1 s per 24 MP export budget."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "perf-hdr-encode-serial-pq",
              "name": "Parallelise the per-pixel PQ/HLG conversion by rows (par_rows or rayon chunks, merging max/sum per chunk), use a lookup table for pq_encode, and pass the borrowed slice to write_image_data instead of copying it.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "encode_png_pq and the planes build in encode_avif_hdr convert pixels in a single-threaded loop that calls pq_encode (two powf) three times per pixel, plus a luminance accumulation and a growing Vec; for a 24 MP HDR export that is roughly 144 M powf calls on one core before the encoder starts. encode_png additionally copies the whole U8 sample buffer with to_vec() (up to ~100 MB for 24 MP RGBA) before handing it to the png writer."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "perf-import-candidates-unvirtualized",
              "name": "Virtualize the candidate list (windowed rows with fixed height) and memoize the row component keyed by candidate and checked state; move form fields into a child component so typing does not re-render the list.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "ImportDialog keeps all review candidates (one per file in the chosen folder or card, unbounded) in component state and renders every one as a <label> with an ImportCandidatePreview in a plain map(). The same component owns the Keywords, Album, Destination, Rename and extension text fields, so each keystroke re-renders the entire candidate list, and each checkbox toggle copies the whole checked[] array and re-renders all rows. Importing a 5,000-photo card makes typing in any field and toggling selections visibly lag."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "perf-library-caches-flushed-by-every-develop-edit",
              "name": "Key aggregate caches on a metadata/membership revision that develop-only ops do not bump, or skip invalidation for ops that touch only develop settings.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Every Caches entry (counts, filter_values, date_groups, folder_tree, keyword_tree, people, album counts) is keyed on cat.revision, which Catalog::apply bumps for every op, including Op::SetDevelop applied during slider drags. Panels that are visible then recompute their O(N) library scans per drag step: counts walks all photos, filter_values clones and sorts every camera and lens string, date_groups and trees rebuild. Edits that cannot affect these aggregates still flush them, so editing a photo in a large library costs several full scans per frame."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "perf-look-extract-retains-all-decoded-samples",
              "name": "Stream: decode each sample, fold it into the look statistics (or a per-sample summary) and drop the buffer, decoding samples on a bounded worker pool; or lower the cap to what fits a fixed memory budget.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "look extract accepts up to 256 sample files and decodes every one to a linear Rgb32f (12 bytes per pixel, up to 1024x1024 = about 12.6 MB each) into the decoded Vec before extract_linear is called on the whole slice, so peak memory is up to about 3.2 GB (about 2 GB for typical 3:2 samples) and every decode is serial. On a modest machine 256 rendered samples exhaust memory or swap even though the statistics only need an accumulator per sample."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "perf-lrcat-row-materialization",
              "name": "Share column names (Arc<str> or index-based rows, or typed row structs) instead of cloning a String per cell, and extract only the needed columns while streaming rows into the per-photo structures.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "`Reader::table` reads every record of a table with `read_table` into memory and then converts each row to a `BTreeMap<String, Value>`. It clones every column name `String` for every row, on top of the whole database and WAL file bytes already held in RAM (up to 4 GiB). For a large Lightroom catalog, `Adobe_images` (about 40 columns) and the history-step and keyword tables, with hundreds of thousands of photos, create tens of millions of small String and BTreeMap node allocations. Resident memory is multiples of the catalog size and import start-up takes minutes. Tables over 1M rows are rejected, which for the optional history and keyword tables silently drops them with only a warning."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "perf-missing-count-restat-on-every-revision",
              "name": "Debounce the revision-triggered refresh (minimum interval regardless of revision, e.g. 5 s) or derive the count from session.media.availability instead of re-statting; avoid cloning all paths on the UI thread.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "missing_count treats the cache as stale whenever catalog.revision differs, and revision increments on every applied op, including Op::SetDevelop. While a slider is dragged (an op per change) each finished scan is immediately followed by another: the UI thread collects every library photo's path (missing::candidates) and a new worker thread stats every original file. On a 100k-photo library, or one on a network share, this is a continuous stat storm for the whole editing session, duplicating the cached Availability probe the app already has. The documented 'at most every 5 s' bound only applies when the revision is unchanged."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "perf-new-smart-album-dialog-queries-every-frame",
              "name": "Route the count through Caches::rules_view (or key a cache on revision and rules) as the edit dialog does.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The NewSmartAlbum dialog calls catalog.query(&rules, ...) (a full-library filter) and rebuilds filter_label on every frame the dialog is shown, uncached. The Edit smart album dialog uses Caches::rules_view keyed on revision and rules precisely to avoid this. With a large library every pointer move or keystroke while the dialog is open re-scans all photos, making the name field laggy."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "perf-prefs-writer-fsync-on-ui-thread",
              "name": "Do the serialisation snapshot on the UI thread but hand the bytes to a background writer thread (coalescing to the latest value), keeping sync_all there; keep the exit-time save synchronous.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "PrefsWriter::tick runs from eframe's logic() every frame and, every 3 s (or immediately when the library path changes), re-serialises the entire UiState with to_vec_pretty, compares it to the last write and, when anything changed, writes it on the UI thread with File::create, write_all, sync_all, rename and a directory sync_all. fsync on a busy disk, antivirus-scanned profile or network home regularly takes tens to hundreds of milliseconds, causing visible frame hitches whenever a panel is resized, a thumbnail slider is dragged or any persisted UI value changes."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "perf-rename-keyword-dialog-scans-library-every-frame",
              "name": "Compute the count once per catalog revision and keyword (cache like Caches::keyword_ticks) instead of per frame.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The RenameKeyword dialog scans every photo and every keyword (is_under) each frame just to show a count. Cost is O(photos x keywords) per frame while typing in the field on a large library."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "perf-rule-matcher-per-photo-invariants",
              "name": "Compile a RuleSet once into a matcher (pre-lowered operand, parsed dates, a single now()/cutoff computed per evaluation pass) and evaluate that per photo; at minimum compute now()/shift_iso once per RuleSet::matches batch rather than once per photo.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Rule::matches re-derives rule-constant data for every photo: lower(value) allocates (even for numeric fields such as rating, via other.to_string().to_lowercase()), date rules re-parse the rule date with rule_date for every photo, and inLast/notInLast call now() (thread-local clone or civil() format), then shift_iso which re-parses the current time and two fixed bound dates and formats twice, and the text rule builds a 14-field joined string plus keywords.join and people().join per photo. Smart albums and filters call this per photo over the whole catalog (Catalog::album_contains and query filtering), so a 100k-photo library with an 'in the last' smart album pays several allocations and four date parses per photo on every re-evaluation."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "perf-run-awaits-full-snapshot",
              "name": "Refresh only after commands that mutate library/session state (or let the host return a revision in the command result and refresh only when it advanced), and do not await the refresh before resolving the command promise.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Every non-UI command routed through run() (including read-only queries such as library.preferences, app.gpu, presets.list, photo.inspect and high-frequency edits) is followed by await refresh(), i.e. a full snapshot round trip through spawn_blocking and the session-owner channel before the caller's promise resolves. Opening Settings alone issues six queries, each chased by a snapshot; a burst of per-tick commands serialises on the snapshot latency instead of the command latency, working against the 16 ms slider budget."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "perf-snapshot-poll-context-churn",
              "name": "Skip setSnapshot when the new snapshot is structurally unchanged (compare a host-supplied revision/generation or a cheap hash), split rapidly changing status (jobs/progress) into its own context or selector so grid/inspector consumers do not subscribe to it, and drop JobDialog's separate interval in favour of the provider's single poll.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DesktopProvider polls getSnapshot every 1200 ms (250 ms while importing/exporting/jobs) and calls setSnapshot(next) unconditionally. The IPC result is always a fresh object, so the single memoized context value (snapshot, ui, dialog, error, ...) changes on every poll and every useDesktop() consumer re-renders even when nothing changed. During a long import/export the cadence is 4 Hz, and JobDialog adds its own 300 ms setInterval that also calls refresh(), so the whole shell re-renders and the host session owner serves a full snapshot several times a second while the user is trying to keep working."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "perf-ui-thread-blocking-host-services",
              "name": "Route the remaining pickers through the threaded PickRequest/picker path and run open_url/reveal on a background task (or spawn without waiting) like open_model_folder, delivering the result through the existing task callback.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The comment at lines 450-451 records that a file dialog on the UI thread stopped the window answering the compositor, which then reported the app as hung (#191), and Services::picker moves its dialogs to a thread. The other services were not moved: pick_folder (used by Browse Folder, Move Folder To, Open Library, import dialogs), pick_files, pick_lightroom_catalog, pick_preset_files, save_preset_file and friends are called synchronously from the egui frame (left.rs:845 calls pick_folder inside the click handler), and open_url / reveal block on Command::status() for xdg-open (which can wait for a freshly launched browser or file manager) while the model-folder opener was already moved to a background task for that reason. Clicking Browse Folder freezes painting and input for as long as the dialog or launcher is open."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "res-close-handler-blocks-main-thread",
              "name": "Give DesktopHandle::snapshot a bounded recv_timeout and treat a timeout as 'busy' (prevent close and emit a notice); move shutdown_process off the event-loop thread (spawn it, then close the window from the completion) so the UI keeps painting.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The CloseRequested branch of on_window_event runs on the main (event-loop) thread and calls host.snapshot(), whose call() waits on reply_rx.recv() with no timeout, and then host.shutdown_process(), which blocks for up to 30 s. If the session-owner thread is busy or wedged (a long synchronous command, a stalled volume, a deadlock), clicking the window close button freezes the entire UI, including the unsaved-changes and 'still running' prompts that depend on that snapshot, with no way to close short of killing the process; even a healthy slow shutdown freezes the window for up to 30 s with no feedback."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "res-dns-resolve-ignores-cancel-and-deadline",
              "name": "Resolve on a helper thread and wait on a channel with the connect timeout, cancel and deadline checks, or document the exception.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Stream::connect resolves the host with to_socket_addrs(), a blocking getaddrinfo call that honours neither the cancel flag, the connect timeout, nor the whole-operation deadline (limits.check() runs only before and after). With a broken resolver or captive network the worker thread blocks for the OS resolver timeout (often tens of seconds) after the user cancels a download or after the 20/45 s deadline of an OpenRouter request, contradicting the module's stated guarantee that a dead server or cancelled download never hangs the thread. Quit-time task waits are held up as well."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "res-fs-read-before-size-cap",
              "name": "Check metadata().len() (and that it is a regular file) first, or read through Read::take(MAX + 1) and fail if the limit is exceeded.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "migrate_preferences and persisted_library_path read the whole file with fs::read and only then compare the length to the 4 MiB cap, on the startup path in setup(). A pathological or corrupt legacy ui.json (multi-GB file, or a symlink to a device/FIFO such as /dev/zero) is fully loaded (or never returns) before being rejected, so a bad settings file can hang or OOM app launch instead of producing the intended 'exceeds N bytes' warning."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "res-hdr-avif-packet-loop-accepts-empty",
              "name": "Continue the loop on EncoderStatus::Encoded, break only on LimitReached, and return Error::Encode when no KEY packet was produced (av1.is_empty()); add a round-trip test for encode_avif_hdr's success path.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The rav1e receive loop treats EncoderStatus::Encoded as the terminal state (break) alongside LimitReached, but rav1e documents Encoded as 'a frame was encoded but no packet emitted yet, call receive_packet again' (ravif's own loop continues on it). If the first call after flush() returns Encoded, av1 stays empty, nothing checks av1.is_empty(), and avif_serialize::Aviffy::to_vec wraps zero bytes of image data in a valid-looking AVIF, so the HDR export reports success while writing an undecodable file (the originals may already be skipped or overwritten per the export conflict setting)."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "res-open-external-child-never-reaped",
              "name": "Wait for the short-lived launcher on a background thread (spawn a thread that calls child.wait() and logs a non-zero status) or use status() inside spawn_blocking.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "open_external spawns the opener (open -a, cmd start, xdg-open) and immediately drops the Child without wait(); Rust does not reap on drop, so on macOS every Edit in External Editor / Show in Folder leaves a defunct 'open' process until Ember exits, and the spawn error path logs nothing about the exit status, so an opener that fails after spawning (editor not found) is reported as success."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "res-png-encode-error-swallowed",
              "name": "Make PngEncode return Result<Vec<u8>, String> and propagate the error through the control reply, or at minimum log the error and return an error reply when the encoded buffer is empty.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The png service wraps encode_png(...).unwrap_or_default(); PngEncode returns Vec<u8>, so an encoding failure (zero or oversize dimensions, png writer error, out of memory) becomes an empty byte vector with no log and no telemetry. ui.screenshot / app.screenshot then pass those bytes to write() and reply ok:true with the path, leaving a zero-byte .png that scripts and CI treat as a successful screenshot (the CLI snapshot prints 'wrote ...' for it too)."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "res-refresh-inflight-never-settles",
              "name": "Add a timeout to the snapshot IPC (reject after a few seconds, clear refreshInFlight in finally as now, and surface the error), and make the host-side recv bounded; do not make command completion wait on the shared in-flight promise.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "refresh() deduplicates through refreshInFlight and returns the stored promise to every caller, and run() awaits refresh() after almost every command. getSnapshot() reaches host.snapshot(), which waits for the session owner with an unbounded recv(). If the owner is wedged (or the snapshot never replies), the in-flight promise never settles, refreshInFlight stays set forever, every later poll and every command awaiting refresh() hangs on the same pending promise, and no error is ever surfaced because neither .then nor .catch runs: the UI stops updating and every menu action appears to do nothing, with no recovery short of restarting."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "res-startup-settings-failures-swallowed-and-marked-applied",
              "name": "Log (and surface) the Err, and only mark the setting applied when execute succeeds.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "apply_settings records gpu_applied and memory_applied before executing app.gpu and app.memoryBudget and discards both results with let _ =. If the engine refuses the command (invalid budget, GPU init error) there is no log line, no toast and no retry, yet the Settings UI shows the value as active; a field report of 'memory budget ignored' leaves zero signal in the persisted log."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "res-windows-launchers-regress-known-issues",
              "name": "Share one launcher implementation with the egui app (rundll32 url.dll,FileProtocolHandler for URLs, raw_arg-quoted /select for reveal, ignore explorer's exit code on Windows).",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The desktop host's reveal() runs explorer with arg(format!(\"/select,{path}\")) and requires a zero exit status. The egui app documents (issues #272 and #234, lightcraft/src/main.rs:395-413 and 385-388) that Rust quotes the whole switch when the path has a space so Explorer opens Documents instead of selecting the file, that the path must be backslashed and quoted via raw_arg, and that explorer exits non-zero even when it worked (hence its 's.success() || cfg!(windows)'). services.rs does none of this, so on Windows Show in Explorer opens the wrong folder for paths with spaces and returns 'reveal failed' when it did work; open_url likewise uses explorer, which the same file documents as opening File Explorer for URLs with a query or fragment."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "resil-geocode-nonatomic-dataset-write",
              "name": "Write each file into `.download` and `std::fs::rename` it into place, with the cities file last.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The doc comment says the new place-name files replace the old ones only when complete, but `run` writes them with `std::fs::write` straight over the live files (states and countries first, then the cities file), not to staging followed by a rename. If the disk fills, the app is killed or a write fails part-way, the live `cities` file is left truncated (size and mtime changed) or the three files come from different downloads. `PlaceNames::load` then re-parses the truncated file and reverse geocoding silently fills wrong or missing places. A previously good dataset is destroyed by a failed refresh, and the user is told only that the download failed."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "resil-import-probe-panic-aborts-scan",
              "name": "Wrap each `probe(..)` call in `crate::guard::catch` and map a panic to `Err(msg)` for that file, as `run_transfers` does.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Import probes run untrusted camera/image files through `probe_bytes` (raw/EXIF/header parsing) on `thread::scope` workers with no per-file panic guard. The copy stage wraps each file in `guard::catch` (import.rs:1552) so one bad file becomes `PreparedItem::Failed`, but the probe stage has no such wrapper. A panic in the parser on one corrupt file kills that worker and `thread::scope` re-panics in the caller after the workers join. The whole scan or import of the folder fails, or on the app's scan worker thread the result is never delivered. The serial branch has the same gap. One damaged file among thousands blocks the import of all the others."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "resil-remote-denoise-permanent-giveup",
              "name": "On an I/O failure of a pooled connection, retry once on a fresh connection before counting a failure, and make the failure count consecutive (reset on success) or time-decayed.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "`Remote` keeps pooled TCP connections open and the server closes idle connections after its 120 s timeout. `run_now` pops a pooled connection and, when the write or reply fails on a connection the server already closed, returns the error with no retry on a fresh connection. `try_run` adds it to `failures`, a cumulative counter that is never reset on success and has no time decay. After an idle gap or a brief network blip, up to `slots` (4) stale pooled connections each fail one tile. After `MAX_FAILURES` = 3 total failures `healthy()` stays false for the life of the loaded model. The remote GPU is permanently abandoned (\"given up\") for the session although the server is fine, and denoise silently runs on the local runner only."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "schema-favorite-themes-strict-voids-ui-json",
              "name": "In the persisted-settings path, drop unknown/duplicate favourite ids (filter) instead of failing, or extend decode_saved_ui's field-level recovery to favoriteThemes as it does for docking; keep strict validation for control-channel writes if desired.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "favoriteThemes uses a deserializer that errors on any id not in ThemeKind::ALL. That error fails deserialization of the whole UiState in decode_saved_ui, which only has a compatibility recovery for `docking`; load_prefs_at then treats ui.json as damaged, renames it aside and starts with defaults, so library_path, recent libraries and the user keymap are lost for the session. A ui.json written by a build with an additional/renamed theme (downgrade, or a theme rename) triggers this from one favourite star. (Load path: apps/lightcraft/src/main.rs:226-277, decode_saved_ui / load_prefs_at.)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "schema-gain-max-deviation-key-not-camelcase",
              "name": "Add `rename_all_fields = \"camelCase\"` to the enum (or `#[serde(rename = \"maxDeviation\", alias = \"max_deviation\")]` on the field) so that existing installed manifests still read.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "`#[serde(rename_all = \"camelCase\")]` on an enum renames only the variant names, not the fields inside struct variants. The JSON key is therefore `max_deviation`, while every other manifest key is camelCase, and the docs (line 34) and the validator error (`gain.maxDeviation`) say `maxDeviation`. A third-party denoise-model.json written as documented, `{\"kind\":\"matchMean\",\"nominal\":1e6,\"maxDeviation\":0.05}`, fails manifest::parse with 'missing field max_deviation', so the model cannot be installed."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "schema-legacy-ui-language-drops-supported-locales",
              "name": "Migrate legacy.language with normalizeLocale(legacy.language) when it maps to a supported LocaleCode, instead of a fixed list. Add a migration test for a pt-br legacy ui.json.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The legacy egui UiState serializes `language` as a Locale code (en, zh-hans, zh-hant, ja, pt-br, es, de, ru, fr, uk). migrateLegacyUi only accepts the hard-coded subset ['en','zh-hans','zh-hant','ja']. A user who chose Portuguese (Brasil) in the egui app, which the React UI also supports (normalizeLocale maps pt-* to pt-br), loses that choice on migration and falls back to English. The migration contract drifted from the Rust Locale serialization it reads."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "schema-manifest-tile-exceeds-remote-limit",
              "name": "Use one shared tile limit: either cap manifest tiles at the remote/GPU limit (1024) or raise remote::MAX_TILE (and its payload bounds) to the manifest's 2048.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Manifest validation accepts tiles up to 2048 cells, but the remote denoise protocol caps tiles at MAX_TILE = 1024, and serve() refuses to start beyond that. A valid model with tile 1536 installs and runs locally, but `lightcraft-cli denoise-serve` fails with 'tile size 1536 is outside 1..=1024', so its remote denoise can never be used. The two contracts disagree on the accepted range."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "schema-p9-lightroom-result-shape-drift",
              "name": "Always store the final result in one shape (e.g. {report, indexWarning?, error?, path}, or always flatten the report and add indexWarning/error alongside) and document it in docs/control-protocol.md; add a test asserting the shape for both clean and index-warning completions.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The final result of library.importLightroom (returned by wait:true and by status polling via lightroom_last) has two shapes for the same outcome: a clean import stores the engine report itself (top-level imported/failed/photos/... plus path), while an import whose archive index write failed or whose commit reported an error stores {report:{...}, indexWarning|error, path}. A control/MCP client reading result.imported gets the count on success but null when only the index write warned, although the photos were added; terminal() papers over this internally with value.get(\"report\").unwrap_or(&value), but external consumers see the drift."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "schema-snapshot-doc-version-drift",
              "name": "Change the comment to reference VERSION (\"version\":V) as the module docs do.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "write_snapshot's contract comment says the catalog.snap bytes are exactly {\"format\":\"lightcraft-catalog\",\"version\":1,...}, but the writer emits version {VERSION} (currently 8). Anyone building a reader or a fixture from this documented byte layout writes version 1, which Journal::open then treats as an older format and rewrites (reported as upgraded_from 1), and a v1-gated external tool would reject real snapshots."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "schema-xmp-dc-creator-seq-collapsed",
              "name": "Keep the creator list structured (e.g. a Vec in Metadata), or split artist on the same \"; \" separator when writing so that read and write are inverses.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "dc:creator is an ordered rdf:Seq of creators. parse_xmp joins every entry into one string (\"Alice; Bob\") and write_xmp_lc writes Metadata.artist back as a single rdf:li. Because Ember's sidecar merge owns dc:creator (always replaced on save), a sidecar from another app with creators [Alice, Bob] is rewritten on Ember's next save as one creator \"Alice; Bob\", even when the user never edited the artist. Other readers then see one creator whose name contains a semicolon."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "security-cr2-slice-width-overflow",
              "name": "Sum the widths with checked_add (as sraw.rs does) and reject any slice width greater than the frame's samples per row before slicing.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The CR2 slice tag (CR2_SLICE) values s[1] and s[2] are taken from the file unbounded (only >0 checked) and summed with an unchecked iter().sum(). A crafted CR2 with, e.g., slices [1, 2^63, 2^63+1] makes the sum wrap to width=1 in release, which passes the total%width check. Mode::Full then executes data[y*width+x0 .. +sw] with sw=2^63 and panics (debug builds panic at the sum). Opening the file in the app crashes the decode thread, or aborts the web build (panic=abort). The sibling sRAW path uses try_fold/checked_add (cr2/sraw.rs:87)."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "security-lossy-dng-jpeg-unbounded-alloc",
              "name": "Call decode_headers() first and reject a JPEG whose dimensions exceed the chunk's cw x ch (or MAX_SAMPLES), or set max_width/max_height to the chunk dimensions.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "For lossy DNG (compression 34892), decode_chunk bounds n=cw*ch*cpp by MAX_SAMPLES, but lossy_jpeg() hands the chunk to zune-jpeg with max width/height 65536 and never compares the JPEG's own frame header with the chunk dimensions before decode(). A tiny crafted DNG whose tile is a JPEG header declaring 65535x65535 RGB (with a truncated body, accepted because strict mode is off) makes the decoder allocate about 12.9 GB of output per tile. Tiles are decoded in parallel, so this exhausts memory or aborts on allocation failure. The only size check (line 239) runs after the allocation."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "security-open-external-windows-cmd-start-injection",
              "name": "Do not route file opening through cmd.exe. Use ShellExecuteW through a maintained opener crate (e.g. the opener/open crate or tauri-plugin-opener), or call `explorer.exe <path>` / `rundll32 url.dll,FileProtocolHandler`, so no shell parsing happens. If cmd must stay, validate the path and pass it with raw_arg using cmd-safe quoting and caret-escaping. Add a regression test with a path that contains `&` and `%`.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "On Windows, with no external editor configured, open_external runs `cmd /C start \"\" <path>` and passes the photo path as an ordinary argument. Rust's Windows argument quoting only wraps arguments that contain a space or tab, and cmd.exe does not follow the MSVC escaping rules, so its metacharacters (&, |, ^, %VAR%) stay live. The path comes from the library: Edit in External Editor (photo.editInExternal -> photo.editExternal result path) sends it through lc_native openExternalEditor. Importing and then editing a photo named like `IMG&calc&.jpg` (no spaces) makes cmd run `calc` as a separate command. Any legitimate filename with an unspaced `&` also fails to open the right file."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "security-remote-preauth-slot-exhaustion",
              "name": "Apply a short pre-authentication deadline (for example a 5 s read timeout until the first valid token line), and only then switch to the full timeout. Optionally cap connections per peer IP.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The denoise server counts a connection against max_connections (default 8) when it is accepted, before any token check, and each connection may stay idle for the full 120 s read timeout. Any host that can reach the port, without knowing the token, can open 8 connections and send nothing, repeating every 2 minutes. Every legitimate client then gets 'server busy', so the remote GPU is denied to its owner. The listener is meant for a LAN/VPN, which limits who can reach it."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "security-unbounded-sidecar-reads-bypass-import-cap",
              "name": "Route read_packet and sidecar_contents through the same bounded reader as import (read_sidecar_packet with MAX_SIDECAR_BYTES) and return an actionable error when the sidecar is larger.",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Import caps a sidecar at MAX_SIDECAR_BYTES (4 MiB, import.rs:648-662), but read_packet (photo.readMetadataFromFile and other re-reads) reads any found .xmp whole with read_to_string, and for raws reads the entire original into memory; sidecar_contents (every save and XMP auto-write) likewise std::fs::read()s the existing sidecar unbounded. A hostile or corrupt multi-GB IMG_0001.xmp next to a photo makes the allocation fail, which aborts the process: a local file turns a metadata command into a crash, against the repo's own 'cap allocations sized by input' rule."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "sha256:004eb5ac37bfbe37001f562bdcb3c4787ce60a2746d056483f9976049cdf8d0a",
              "name": "Audit finding",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Provider reported a finding"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "sha256:006ebae1ac2bbd65f85650d88dbcb0e817b4a4ffe19139f3ba25e1badbcaaa17",
              "name": "Audit finding",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Provider reported a finding"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "sha256:01ed5f111579b8a8676196897a27d8195d73d3492616991d8f3ff0d5c1ba4439",
              "name": "Audit finding",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Provider reported a finding"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "sha256:029697013a42e271fc1eecb905da122e3bd8464690ec94d39d4deb6b297a7941",
              "name": "Audit finding",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Provider reported a finding"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "sha256:02eb06c46463ba4e60dd47101ebca2213ab09e3369ac3bd9b078cd47a279d99b",
              "name": "Audit finding",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Provider reported a finding"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "sha256:034b4ef4e6063d73ce49f20920afae6683fc16019135e805718b54bd016b4f2b",
              "name": "Audit finding",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Provider reported a finding"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "sha256:03635cf065428c06ba2e583575dd9e318b3866f5e0c882aa7e5830c4d2cedb7d",
              "name": "Audit finding",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Provider reported a finding"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "sha256:03707d2b10b75a3b2140062fd69fdf7557702af7d2a4cf3a4751c79302af5380",
              "name": "Audit finding",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Provider reported a finding"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
