          "ruleId": "arch-session-catalog-public-bypasses-ops",
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "Make Session::catalog read-only to frontends (pub(crate) field plus a &Catalog accessor) and route non-user bookkeeping through an engine API such as apply_system/commit so the documented ops-only invariant is enforced by visibility, not convention.: lib.rs:7 documents the Catalog as 'mutated only by ops, so every change is undoable and journaled', but Session exposes it as a public mutable field. A frontend already bypasses it: desktop-host controller.rs:73 calls session.catalog.apply(Op::RemovePhoto) directly when --demo-count trims an opened library. That removal is not pushed to pending_log, not on the undo stack and skips apply_catalog's media.forget, so the in-memory catalog and the library's op log diverge (the photos come back on reopen, or a later snapshot/compaction persists the removal with no undo step)."
