          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "Make the field tolerant: deserialize Vec<String>, then drop unknown ids and duplicates (as the keymap does) instead of returning an error. Keep strict validation for the control-channel write path if that is where it is wanted.: favorite_themes uses a validating deserializer that returns an error for any unknown or duplicate theme id. An error in one field fails deserialization of the whole UiState. decode_saved_ui recovers only the docking field (main.rs:226-240), so load_prefs_at treats the whole ui.json as damaged: the file is renamed to ui.json.corrupt-* and every setting reverts to defaults, including library path, keymap, panel layout and appearance (main.rs:263-279). This happens when a ui.json written by a build with an extra or renamed theme id is opened by another build (downgrade, web vs native, or a theme removed later), or when the file is hand-edited. That is the opposite of the persistence policy the same file follows for the keymap, where a bad entry is ignored (shortcuts.rs:54-57)."
