          "ruleId": "p0006-correctness-sync-undo-interleave-at-cap",
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Track interleaving with the monotonic s.commits() counter (record commits after each own step and flag interleaved when it differs on entry) plus an undo-pop counter, instead of undo.len().: SyncCommit detects an Undo or edit made between its own steps only by comparing undo.len() with the length after its last step. Session::commit caps the undo stack at 1000 (lib.rs:516 removes the oldest entry), so once the history is full every commit leaves undo.len() unchanged; likewise an Undo followed by one edit restores the same length. In the app the SyncWork runs on a worker while the UI thread keeps accepting edits: with a full history, import batch committed -> user edits a photo -> Relink step committed, after_last == undo.len() so interleaved stays false, and finish() merges the last `own` (2) entries = [user edit, relink] into one 'Synchronize Folder' step. Undoing Synchronize Folder then also reverts the user's unrelated edit, contrary to the documented contract that an edit made meanwhile stays its own step."
          },
